INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4367,'Angela Mkrtchyan','angela.mkrtchyan@wellsfargo.com','''+1 (310) 916-7250','Wells Fargo',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/angela-mkrtchyan-83a3b3120',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-03T17:41:01.879886','2025-12-03T17:41:01.879887',NULL,NULL,NULL,'323-819-6716','SBA Lending at Wells Fargo','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.879885',0,NULL,unistr('=== PERSON RESEARCH: Angela Mkrtchyan ===\u000a\u000aBased on the available search results, I can provide limited information about Angela Mkrtchyan:\u000a\u000a## Current Role and Location\u000a\u000aAngela Mkrtchyan holds a position in commercial lending at Wells Fargo[1][2]. According to the search results, she is based in the Los Angeles/Glendale, California area and serves as a VP/Commercial Lending Area Manager[2].\u000a\u000a## Career History and Education\u000a\u000aThe search results indicate that Angela Mkrtchyan attended an educational institution between 1996 and 1999[1], though the specific school name and degree are not provided in the available information.\u000a\u000a## Limitations\u000a\u000aUnfortunately, the search results do not contain comprehensive information about the following aspects of Angela Mkrtchyan''s professional profile:\u000a\u000a- Specific tenure dates at Wells Fargo or detailed responsibilities in her current role\u000a- Complete career history with previous positions and employers\u000a- Specific degrees, credentials, or certifications\u000a- Professional achievements, awards, or recognition\u000a- LinkedIn activity, posts, or thought leadership content\u000a- Speaking engagements, podcasts, or media appearances\u000a- Professional associations, board positions, or affiliations\u000a- Management style or professional philosophy\u000a- Notable deals, projects, or specific accomplishments\u000a\u000aTo obtain comprehensive information about Angela Mkrtchyan''s complete professional background, career trajectory, and achievements, you would need to access her LinkedIn profile directly, contact Wells Fargo''s public relations department, or search for additional professional databases and industry publications that may contain more detailed biographical information.\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH: Wells Fargo ===\u000a\u000a### 1. Company Overview\u000a\u000a**Founding Date:**  \u000aWells Fargo & Company was founded on March 18, 1852, in San Francisco, California.\u000a\u000a**Headquarters:**  \u000aWells Fargo’s corporate headquarters is located at 420 Montgomery Street, San Francisco, California, 94104, USA.\u000a\u000a**Employee Count:**  \u000aAs of 2025, Wells Fargo employs approximately 230,000 people globally. The company has reduced its workforce from over 265,000 in 2020 as part of ongoing efficiency initiatives and business simplification.\u000a\u000a**Revenue:**  \u000a- In 2024, Wells Fargo generated $82.3 billion in total revenue.  \u000a- For the third quarter of 2025, revenue increased 6% year-over-year, driven by growth in net interest income, lower deposit pricing, and higher deposit and loan balances.  \u000a- For full-year 2025, noninterest expense is projected at approximately $54.6 billion.\u000a\u000a**Assets:**  \u000a- As of mid-2025, Wells Fargo’s total consolidated assets are approximately $1.9 trillion to $2.1 trillion, depending on the reporting period and methodology.  \u000a- The company ranked No. 33 on Fortune’s 2025 list of America’s largest corporations and fourth in assets among U.S. banks.\u000a\u000a**Stock Information:**  \u000a- Wells Fargo & Company is publicly traded on the New York Stock Exchange under the ticker symbol WFC.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 2. Business Model and How They Make Money\u000a\u000aWells Fargo operates as a diversified financial services company with a multi-segment business model. The company generates revenue through:\u000a\u000a- **Net Interest Income:** Earned from the spread between interest paid on deposits and interest received on loans and investments.\u000a- **Noninterest Income:** Includes fees from banking services, investment banking, wealth management, mortgage banking, and capital markets activities.\u000a- **Fee-Based Services:** Revenue from transaction fees, account maintenance, payment processing, and advisory services.\u000a- **Capital Markets Activities:** Income from trading, underwriting, and advisory services in equities, fixed income, commodities, foreign exchange, and credit products.\u000a- **Wealth and Investment Management:** Fees from asset management, brokerage, and retirement services.\u000a\u000aWells Fargo’s business model emphasizes scale, diversification, and operational efficiency. The company has focused on simplifying its operations, exiting non-core businesses, and investing in core franchises to improve profitability and return on equity.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 3. Products, Services, and Offerings\u000a\u000aWells Fargo offers a comprehensive suite of financial products and services across four main operating segments:\u000a\u000a**Consumer Banking and Lending:**  \u000a- Checking and savings accounts  \u000a- Credit and debit cards  \u000a- Personal loans and lines of credit  \u000a- Auto loans  \u000a- Student lending (sold portfolio in 2025)  \u000a- Home equity loans and lines of credit\u000a\u000a**Commercial Banking:**  \u000a- Business banking services  \u000a- Commercial real estate lending and servicing  \u000a- Treasury management  \u000a- Payment solutions\u000a\u000a**Corporate and Investment Banking (CIB):**  \u000a- Corporate banking  \u000a- Investment banking (M&A, underwriting, advisory)  \u000a- Treasury management  \u000a- Commercial real estate lending and servicing  \u000a- Equity and fixed income solutions  \u000a- Sales, trading, and research capabilities\u000a\u000a**Wealth and Investment Management:**  \u000a- Brokerage services  \u000a- Asset management  \u000a- Retirement planning  \u000a- Trust and estate services  \u000a- Financial advisory\u000a\u000a**Other Offerings:**  \u000a- Mortgage banking (focus on existing banking and wealth management customers)  \u000a- Insurance products  \u000a- Payment processing and merchant services\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 4. Target Markets and Customer Segments\u000a\u000aWells Fargo serves a broad range of customer segments:\u000a\u000a- **Individual Consumers:** Personal banking, credit cards, auto loans, mortgages, and wealth management.\u000a- **Small and Medium Businesses:** Business banking, lending, and treasury management.\u000a- **Large Corporations and Institutions:** Corporate banking, investment banking, capital markets, and commercial real estate.\u000a- **Government and Institutional Clients:** Treasury management, investment banking, and advisory services.\u000a- **Wealth Management Clients:** High-net-worth individuals, families, and institutions.\u000a\u000aThe company’s primary market is the United States, with a significant presence in consumer, small business, and commercial banking. Wells Fargo also serves global clients through its corporate and investment banking division.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 5. Competitive Landscape\u000a\u000a**Key Competitors:**  \u000a- JPMorgan Chase  \u000a- Bank of America  \u000a- Citigroup  \u000a- U.S. Bank  \u000a- PNC Financial Services  \u000a- Goldman Sachs (for investment banking and wealth management)\u000a\u000a**Differentiation:**  \u000a- Scale and breadth of products and services  \u000a- Strong retail and commercial banking presence  \u000a- Diversified revenue streams  \u000a- Focus on efficiency and cost management  \u000a- Investment in technology and digital capabilities  \u000a- Commitment to risk management and regulatory compliance\u000a\u000aWells Fargo has gained market share in investment banking, with a 120 basis point increase in U.S. investment banking share since 2022. The company is recognized as a top 5 U.S. investment bank and a leading wealth manager.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 6. Recent News – Funding, Acquisitions, Leadership Changes, Strategic Moves\u000a\u000a**Strategic Moves:**  \u000a- Sold or exited several non-core businesses between 2019 and 2025, including Institutional Retirement & Trust, Real Estate Investment Banking, Asset Management, Corporate Trust Services, Canadian Direct Equipment Finance, Student Lending portfolio, and Norwest Equity Partners and Mezzanine Partners funds.\u000a- Simplified business structure to focus on core franchises and improve earnings profile.\u000a- Invested in senior talent, improved capabilities and infrastructure, and reduced attrition through improved offerings and advisor experience.\u000a\u000a**Funding and Capital Return:**  \u000a- Repurchased $6.1 billion of common stock in the third quarter of 2025.\u000a- Maintained a strong capital position with a CET1 ratio of 11.0% under the Standardized Approach as of June 30, 2025.\u000a- Liquidity coverage ratio (LCR) of 121% as of June 30, 2025.\u000a\u000a**Leadership Changes:**  \u000a- No major leadership changes reported in 2025. The company continues to be led by CEO Charles Scharf, who has been in the role since October 2019.\u000a\u000a**Other News:**  \u000a- The lifting of the Federal Reserve’s asset cap in 2025 allowed Wells Fargo to pursue growth opportunities and capital return.\u000a- The company has made significant progress in diversifying its revenue mix and growing fee income streams.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 7. Company Culture, Values, and Mission\u000a\u000a**Mission:**  \u000a“To help our customers succeed financially.”\u000a\u000a**Values:**  \u000a- Customer focus  \u000a- Integrity  \u000a- Teamwork  \u000a- Diversity and inclusion  \u000a- Community involvement\u000a\u000a**Culture:**  \u000aWells Fargo emphasizes a culture of accountability, risk management, and regulatory compliance. The company has made significant efforts to rebuild trust and improve its reputation following past scandals. Initiatives include enhanced risk and control frameworks, improved employee training, and a focus on ethical conduct.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 8. Leadership Team and Organizational Structure\u000a\u000a**Leadership Team:**  \u000a- **Charles Scharf:** Chief Executive Officer  \u000a- **John Shrewsberry:** Chief Financial Officer  \u000a- **Mary Mack:** CEO of Wealth & Investment Management  \u000a- **Scott Powell:** CEO of Consumer Banking and Lending  \u000a- **Michael Loughlin:** CEO of Commercial Banking  \u000a- **Blythe McGarvie:** Lead Independent Director\u000a\u000a**Organizational Structure:**  \u000aWells Fargo is organized into four main operating segments:  \u000a- Consumer Banking and Lending  \u000a- Commercial Banking  \u000a- Corporate and Investment Banking  \u000a- Wealth and Investment Management\u000a\u000aEach segment is led by a dedicated CEO, with centralized functions for risk management, compliance, technology, and operations.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 9. Technology Stack and Innovations\u000a\u000a**Technology Stack:**  \u000a- Core banking platforms for retail and commercial banking  \u000a- Digital banking and mobile apps  \u000a- Payment processing systems  \u000a- Data analytics and artificial intelligence for risk management and customer insights  \u000a- Cloud infrastructure for scalability and security\u000a\u000a**Innovations:**  \u000a- Investment in digital capabilities to enhance customer experience and operational efficiency  \u000a- Development of new digital products and services, including mobile banking, online lending, and digital wealth management  \u000a- Use of data analytics and AI to improve risk management, fraud detection, and customer segmentation  \u000a- Focus on cybersecurity and regulatory compliance\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 10. Market Position, Growth Trajectory, and Industry Standing\u000a\u000a**Market Position:**  \u000a- Fourth largest bank in the U.S. by assets  \u000a- Leading provider of consumer and small business banking services  \u000a- Top 5 U.S. investment bank  \u000a- Major player in wealth management and commercial banking\u000a\u000a**Growth Trajectory:**  \u000a- Revenue increased 6% in the third quarter of 2025, driven by growth in net interest income and fee-based services.  \u000a- Loan growth accelerated, with strong growth in auto loan originations and mortgage banking.  \u000a- The company is targeting a return on tangible common equity (ROTCE) of 17% to 18% over the medium term.\u000a\u000a**Industry Standing:**  \u000a- Ranked No. 33 on Fortune’s 2025 list of America’s largest corporations  \u000a- Fourth in assets and third in market value of common stock among U.S. banks  \u000a- Recognized for operational efficiency, risk management, and regulatory compliance\u000a\u000aWells Fargo continues to focus on simplifying its business, improving efficiency, and investing in core franchises to drive sustainable growth and shareholder value.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE ===\u000a\u000aTo analyze the sales opportunity for reaching Angela Mkrtchyan, SBA Lending at Wells Fargo, here is a detailed breakdown addressing your 10 research points with actionable intelligence:\u000a\u000a1. **Industry Trends Affecting Wells Fargo’s Sector (SBA Lending/Small Business Finance)**\u000a   - SBA 7(a) lending is experiencing near-record high volumes in 2025, driven by a surge in small-dollar loans under $150,000, which now constitute over half of all SBA 7(a) loans. This reflects strong borrower demand for both small working capital and larger expansion financing[1].\u000a   - The SBA lending market is robust post-pandemic, with sustained entrepreneurial activity and cautious borrowing behavior.\u000a   - Wells Fargo is a preferred SBA lender, actively approving a significant volume of SBA 7(a) loans (over 1,600 loans worth nearly $540 million in FY2025 so far), focusing on SBA Express loans with fast approval times[2][3].\u000a\u000a2. **Common Pain Points for SBA Lending Roles at Wells Fargo**\u000a   - Managing high volumes of small-dollar loans efficiently while maintaining underwriting quality.\u000a   - Balancing stringent eligibility criteria (credit scores, time in business, revenue) with the need to serve a diverse small business client base[6].\u000a   - Navigating borrower expectations for fast approvals and flexible loan products amid discontinued term loan offerings.\u000a   - Handling personal guarantee requirements, which can complicate borrower acceptance[6].\u000a\u000a3. **Regulatory or Compliance Challenges**\u000a   - Compliance with SBA program rules, including loan size caps, borrower eligibility, and documentation standards.\u000a   - Adherence to Basel III capital requirements and risk-weighted asset calculations, impacting capital allocation for SBA loans[9].\u000a   - Ensuring anti-fraud measures and accurate reporting to SBA given the high volume of loans and government backing.\u000a   - Managing evolving regulatory scrutiny on small business lending practices and fair lending compliance.\u000a\u000a4. **Technology Trends Impacting Their Business**\u000a   - Increasing use of digital platforms for loan application, underwriting, and management, including integration with accounting software like QuickBooks for real-time financial data access[2].\u000a   - Automation and AI-driven credit risk assessment tools to speed up SBA Express loan approvals.\u000a   - Online portals enabling 24/7 loan management and borrower self-service, improving customer experience and operational efficiency[2].\u000a   - Potential adoption of blockchain or secure digital identity verification to enhance compliance and reduce fraud risk.\u000a\u000a5. **Economic Factors Affecting Decision-Making**\u000a   - Interest rate environment: SBA loan rates range from approximately 11.5% to 18%, influenced by Federal Reserve policy and market conditions[2].\u000a   - Small business economic health and cash flow stability, especially in sectors like construction, food service, and healthcare, which are active SBA loan markets[5].\u000a   - Regional economic growth patterns, such as rapid expansion in markets like Phoenix, affecting loan demand and risk profiles[5].\u000a   - Inflation and supply chain pressures impacting borrower repayment capacity and loan structuring.\u000a\u000a6. **Recent Company Challenges or Opportunities in the News**\u000a   - Wells Fargo continues to face reputational challenges from past compliance issues but is focusing on rebuilding trust through enhanced customer service and digital innovation.\u000a   - The bank is capitalizing on the SBA lending boom by expanding SBA Express loan offerings and streamlining approval processes to capture market share[2][6].\u000a   - Discontinuation of some term loan products may push Wells Fargo to innovate in lines of credit and SBA-backed financing solutions[6].\u000a\u000a7. **Typical Buying Triggers for This Persona Type (SBA Lending Officer)**\u000a   - Need to improve loan processing speed and reduce manual underwriting workload.\u000a   - Demand for technology solutions that enhance borrower experience and compliance tracking.\u000a   - Pressure to increase SBA loan volume while managing risk and regulatory requirements.\u000a   - Desire to differentiate Wells Fargo’s SBA offerings through competitive rates, flexible terms, and digital tools.\u000a\u000a8. **Budget Cycles and Procurement Patterns**\u000a   - As a large national bank, Wells Fargo likely follows annual budget cycles aligned with fiscal years, with procurement decisions for technology and services typically planned in Q3/Q4 for implementation in the following year.\u000a   - Procurement for SBA lending support tools may be centralized but influenced by regional SBA lending volume and strategic priorities.\u000a   - Vendor selection emphasizes compliance, security, scalability, and integration with existing banking platforms.\u000a\u000a9. **Key Initiatives Companies Like Wells Fargo Are Prioritizing**\u000a   - Expanding SBA loan portfolios, especially SBA Express loans, to meet growing small business demand[2].\u000a   - Enhancing digital lending platforms for faster approvals and better borrower engagement.\u000a   - Strengthening compliance frameworks to meet evolving regulatory standards and rebuild customer trust.\u000a   - Supporting underserved markets and women-owned businesses, as highlighted in Wells Fargo’s 2025 impact reports[7].\u000a   - Improving operational efficiency through automation and data analytics.\u000a\u000a10. **Competitive Pressures They’re Facing**\u000a    - Competition from other large banks like Bank of America and JPMorgan Chase, which also have strong SBA lending programs and digital capabilities[2][4].\u000a    - Digital-first SBA lenders like Live Oak Bank offering fully online, efficient loan application processes and industry-specific programs[4].\u000a    - Fintech and alternative lenders providing faster, more flexible small business financing options.\u000a    - Pressure to maintain competitive SBA loan rates and approval times while managing risk and regulatory compliance.\u000a\u000a**Actionable Sales Intelligence:**\u000a\u000a- Emphasize solutions that **accelerate SBA loan processing** and improve underwriting accuracy, especially for small-dollar loans under $150,000.\u000a- Highlight **digital integration capabilities** with Wells Fargo’s existing platforms (e.g., QuickBooks integration, 24/7 loan management).\u000a- Address compliance and regulatory risk management with tools that ensure **SBA program adherence and Basel III capital efficiency**.\u000a- Offer analytics and automation that help balance **volume growth with credit quality**.\u000a- Position offerings to support Wells Fargo’s **key initiatives** around expanding SBA Express loans and enhancing borrower experience.\u000a- Be aware of Wells Fargo’s **budget cycle** and procurement rigor; tailor proposals to align with their fiscal planning and risk management priorities.\u000a- Differentiate by demonstrating how your solution can help Wells Fargo **compete effectively against both traditional banks and digital-first SBA lenders**.\u000a\u000aThis comprehensive profile should enable a targeted, informed sales engagement with Angela Mkrtchyan and the SBA Lending team at Wells Fargo.\u000a\u000a\u000aI''m unable to provide a detailed personality analysis for Angela Mkrtchyan due to the limited information available in the research. However, I can offer a speculative analysis based on her role and the context of her work environment.\u000a\u000a=== PERSONALITY ANALYSIS ===\u000a\u000a## Myers-Briggs (MBTI) Assessment\u000a\u000a**Inferred Type:** ESTJ  \u000a**Confidence:** Low\u000a\u000a| Dimension | Preference | Evidence from Research |\u000a|-----------|------------|----------------------|\u000a| Energy | E - Extraversion | As a VP/Commercial Lending Area Manager, Angela likely engages with various stakeholders, which suggests a preference for extraversion. |\u000a| Information | S - Sensing | Working in commercial lending requires attention to detail and dealing with concrete data, indicating a sensing preference. |\u000a| Decisions | T - Thinking | The role involves making objective decisions based on financial data, suggesting a thinking preference. |\u000a| Structure | J - Judging | A managerial position often requires planning, organization, and decision-making, which aligns with a judging preference. |\u000a\u000a**Work Style:** Likely approaches work with a focus on efficiency, organization, and clear structure, valuing data-driven decision-making and practical solutions.\u000a\u000a## DISC Profile Assessment\u000a\u000a**Primary Style:** D - Dominance  \u000a**Secondary Style:** C - Conscientiousness\u000a\u000a| Style | Estimated % | Evidence |\u000a|-------|-------------|----------|\u000a| D - Dominance | 40% | As a leader in commercial lending, Angela likely exhibits direct, results-oriented behavior, focusing on achieving goals and driving performance. |\u000a| I - Influence | 15% | Limited evidence of people-oriented behavior, as the role seems more task-focused. |\u000a| S - Steadiness | 20% | Some steadiness may be present due to the need for reliability in managing lending operations. |\u000a| C - Conscientiousness | 25% | The role''s analytical and detail-oriented nature suggests a conscientious approach to work. |\u000a\u000a## Communication Playbook\u000a\u000a### ✅ DO: How to Engage This Person\u000a- Be direct and concise in communication.\u000a- Present data and evidence to support your points.\u000a- Focus on efficiency and results when discussing projects or ideas.\u000a\u000a### ❌ DON''T: What to Avoid\u000a- Avoid vague or overly emotional appeals.\u000a- Do not present ideas without a clear plan or supporting data.\u000a- Steer clear of disorganized or last-minute changes.\u000a\u000a### 🎯 Best Opening Approach\u000aStart with a clear agenda and objectives, highlighting how your proposal aligns with organizational goals and efficiency improvements.'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-04T13:56:11.138372','Angela','Mkrtchyan',NULL,'','Los Angeles','California','Financial Services',0,'232951',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
