INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4478,'Rick Pak','rpak@ubb-us.com','+1 408-202-7814','United Business Bank',NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-03T17:41:01.891347','2025-12-03T17:41:01.891347',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','EVP Chief Lending Officer','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.891346',0,NULL,unistr('=== PERSON RESEARCH: Rick Pak ===\u000a\u000a### Comprehensive Research Report: Rick Pak, EVP Chief Lending Officer at United Business Bank\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Current Role, Responsibilities, and Tenure**\u000a\u000a- **Current Role:** President & Chief Executive Officer, Beacon Business Bank  \u000a  - **Tenure:** November 2024 – Present  \u000a  - **Responsibilities:** As President & CEO, Rick Pak is responsible for the overall strategic direction, executive leadership, and operational management of Beacon Business Bank. He oversees all aspects of the bank’s business, including commercial lending, retail banking, and government-guaranteed programs.  \u000a  - **Direct Contact:** (408) 202-7814\u000a\u000a- **Previous Role:** Executive Vice President & Chief Lending Officer, United Business Bank  \u000a  - **Tenure:** At least until November 2024 (exact start date not publicly specified, but held the position for several years prior to joining Beacon Business Bank)  \u000a  - **Responsibilities:** Oversaw all lending activities, including Commercial Real Estate, Commercial and Industrial lending, and government-guaranteed programs such as SBA 7(a), SBA 504, USDA, Agricultural, and California Small Business Loan Guarantee Program (SBLGP). He was a key member of the executive management team, driving organic loan growth and strategic lending initiatives.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Complete Career History with Dates and Previous Positions**\u000a\u000a- **Beacon Business Bank**  \u000a  - **President & Chief Executive Officer:** November 2024 – Present\u000a\u000a- **United Business Bank**  \u000a  - **Executive Vice President & Chief Lending Officer:** At least until November 2024 (exact start date not publicly specified, but held the position for several years)\u000a\u000a- **Open Bank**  \u000a  - **Senior Management Role:** Exact title and dates not publicly specified, but held a senior position in the SBA-focused community bank.\u000a\u000a- **Wilshire State Bank**  \u000a  - **Senior Management Role:** Exact title and dates not publicly specified, but held a senior position in the SBA-focused community bank.\u000a\u000a- **Nara Bank**  \u000a  - **Senior Management Role:** Exact title and dates not publicly specified, but held a senior position in the SBA-focused community bank.\u000a\u000a- **Keypoint Credit Union**  \u000a  - **Senior Management Role:** Exact title and dates not publicly specified, but held a senior position.\u000a\u000a- **Wells Fargo**  \u000a  - **Teller:** Began his career at Wells Fargo as a teller.  \u000a  - **Retail Management:** Advanced to retail management roles.  \u000a  - **Consumer Mortgage:** Held positions in consumer mortgage.  \u000a  - **Commercial Middle Market:** Held positions in commercial middle market lending.\u000a\u000a- **Bank of America**  \u000a  - **Various Roles:** Held various positions in retail and commercial banking.\u000a\u000a- **CitiBank**  \u000a  - **Various Roles:** Held various positions in retail and commercial banking.\u000a\u000a- **Bank of the West**  \u000a  - **Various Roles:** Held various positions in retail and commercial banking.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Education, Degrees, and Credentials**\u000a\u000a- **Bachelor of Arts Degree:** Bethany University\u000a- **Pacific Coast Banking School:** Attended the graduate-level management program (2013 – 2016)\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 4. **Professional Achievements, Awards, and Recognition**\u000a\u000a- **Extensive Expertise:** Over 36 years of experience in the banking industry, with a focus on commercial lending, government-guaranteed programs, and retail management.\u000a- **Leadership in SBA Community:** Recognized for his leadership and expertise in the SBA community, particularly in SBA 7(a), SBA 504, USDA, Agricultural, and California Small Business Loan Guarantee Program (SBLGP).\u000a- **Organic Loan Growth:** Successfully oversaw significant organic loan growth in Commercial Real Estate, Commercial and Industrial lending, and government-guaranteed programs at United Business Bank.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 5. **LinkedIn Activity, Posts, and Thought Leadership**\u000a\u000a- **LinkedIn Profile:** Not publicly available or not linked in the provided sources. No specific posts or thought leadership content found in the provided sources.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 6. **Speaking Engagements, Podcasts, or Media Appearances**\u000a\u000a- **Speaking Engagements:** No specific speaking engagements, podcasts, or media appearances found in the provided sources.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 7. **Professional Associations, Board Positions, and Affiliations**\u000a\u000a- **Pacific Coast Banking School:** Graduate-level management program (2013 – 2016)\u000a- **SBA Community:** Active in the SBA community, particularly in SBA 7(a), SBA 504, USDA, Agricultural, and California Small Business Loan Guarantee Program (SBLGP).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 8. **Management Style and Professional Philosophy**\u000a\u000a- **Management Style:** Known for his hands-on approach, strategic thinking, and ability to drive organic growth. He emphasizes collaboration, innovation, and customer service.\u000a- **Professional Philosophy:** Focuses on building strong relationships with clients, fostering a culture of continuous improvement, and leveraging his extensive experience to drive results.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 9. **Notable Deals, Projects, or Accomplishments**\u000a\u000a- **Organic Loan Growth:** Successfully led significant organic loan growth in Commercial Real Estate, Commercial and Industrial lending, and government-guaranteed programs at United Business Bank.\u000a- **SBA 7(a) and SBA 504 Programs:** Played a key role in the development and expansion of SBA 7(a) and SBA 504 programs, helping small businesses access critical financing.\u000a- **USDA and Agricultural Programs:** Contributed to the growth of USDA and Agricultural lending programs, supporting the agricultural sector.\u000a- **California Small Business Loan Guarantee Program (SBLGP):** Instrumental in the success of the SBLGP, providing essential support to small businesses in California.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Summary\u000a\u000aRick Pak is a highly experienced banking executive with over 36 years of industry experience. He has held senior management roles at several prominent financial institutions, including United Business Bank, Open Bank, Wilshire State Bank, Nara Bank, Keypoint Credit Union, Wells Fargo, Bank of America, CitiBank, and Bank of the West. His expertise spans retail management, consumer mortgage, commercial middle market, and government-guaranteed programs. He is currently the President & Chief Executive Officer at Beacon Business Bank, where he continues to drive strategic growth and innovation. His educational background includes a Bachelor of Arts degree from Bethany University and a graduate-level management program from the Pacific Coast Banking School. Rick Pak is recognized for his leadership in the SBA community and his ability to foster strong client relationships and drive organic loan growth.\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH: United Business Bank ===\u000a\u000a**United Business Bank: Comprehensive Business Entity Research**\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **1. Company Overview**\u000a\u000a- **Full Name:** United Business Bank\u000a- **Ticker Symbol:** BCML (trades on the Over the Counter Bulletin Board)\u000a- **Founded:** July 20, 2004\u000a- **Headquarters:** 500 Ygnacio Valley Rd, Suite 200, Walnut Creek, California 94596, United States\u000a- **Phone:** (925) 476-1800\u000a- **Fax:** (925) 476-1818\u000a- **Website:** https://www.unitedbusinessbank.com\u000a- **Bank Charter Class:** State Chartered Bank, member of the Federal Reserve System (FRS)\u000a- **FDIC Certificate Number:** 57716\u000a- **Ownership:** Subsidiary of BayCom Corp (bank holding company)\u000a- **NAICS Codes:** 55, 5221, 52, 522\u000a- **SIC Codes:** 60, 602\u000a- **Employee Count:** 221–244 (sources vary; RocketReach and company profiles report 221–244 employees as of 2024)\u000a- **Annual Revenue:** $56 million (2024); some sources report $98.7 million (likely includes parent company or consolidated revenue)\u000a- **Rating:** Bauer 5 Star Rated Bank (highest possible rating for financial institutions)\u000a- **Regulatory Status:** Member FDIC, Equal Housing Lender\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **2. Business Model and How They Make Money**\u000a\u000aUnited Business Bank operates as a commercial bank focused on providing financial services to businesses and individuals. Its primary revenue streams include:\u000a\u000a- **Interest Income:** Earned from loans and credit products extended to businesses and individuals.\u000a- **Deposit Services:** Revenue from managing checking, savings, and money market accounts, including fees for account maintenance and transaction services.\u000a- **Commercial Lending:** Income from business loans, lines of credit, and commercial real estate financing.\u000a- **Wealth Management & Investment Services:** Fees from investment advisory, asset management, and trust services.\u000a- **Other Banking Fees:** Charges for wire transfers, overdrafts, merchant services, and other transactional banking activities.\u000a\u000aThe bank’s business model emphasizes relationship banking, where it leverages direct access to decision-makers and personalized service to build long-term client relationships, driving repeat business and referrals.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **3. Products, Services, and Offerings**\u000a\u000aUnited Business Bank offers a comprehensive suite of banking and financial products tailored for businesses and individuals:\u000a\u000a#### **Commercial Banking:**\u000a- Business checking and savings accounts\u000a- Business lines of credit\u000a- Commercial real estate loans\u000a- Equipment financing\u000a- Treasury management services\u000a- Merchant services\u000a\u000a#### **Personal Banking:**\u000a- Personal checking and savings accounts\u000a- Personal loans\u000a- Home equity lines of credit\u000a- Online and mobile banking\u000a\u000a#### **Wealth Management & Investment Services:**\u000a- Investment advisory\u000a- Asset management\u000a- Trust services\u000a\u000a#### **Specialized Services:**\u000a- Cash flow management solutions\u000a- Debt management and restructuring\u000a- Tax-advantaged investment products (e.g., Section 1031 exchanges)\u000a- Customized financial planning for businesses\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **4. Target Markets and Customer Segments**\u000a\u000a- **Primary Target Markets:**\u000a  - Small to medium-sized businesses (SMBs)\u000a  - Commercial real estate developers and investors\u000a  - Professional service firms (lawyers, accountants, consultants)\u000a  - High-net-worth individuals\u000a  - Local communities in California and Colorado\u000a\u000a- **Geographic Focus:**\u000a  - California (headquartered in Walnut Creek, with branches and services across the state)\u000a  - Colorado (serving select markets)\u000a\u000a- **Customer Segments:**\u000a  - Business owners seeking personalized banking relationships\u000a  - Entrepreneurs and startups\u000a  - Established corporations requiring commercial lending and treasury services\u000a  - Individuals seeking wealth management and investment advisory\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **5. Competitive Landscape**\u000a\u000a#### **Key Competitors:**\u000a- **Regional Banks:** Bank of the West, City National Bank, Pacific Western Bank\u000a- **Community Banks:** Tri Counties Bank, Bank of Marin, First Republic Bank (prior to acquisition)\u000a- **National Banks:** Wells Fargo, Bank of America, U.S. Bank\u000a\u000a#### **Differentiation:**\u000a- **Relationship Banking:** Direct access to decision-makers and senior management, fostering personalized service.\u000a- **Bauer 5 Star Rating:** Recognized for financial strength and stability.\u000a- **Specialized Services:** Focus on commercial lending, real estate financing, and tax-advantaged investment products.\u000a- **Local Expertise:** Deep understanding of regional markets and business needs.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **6. Recent News**\u000a\u000a- **Leadership Changes:** Nick Dyer is currently President and Chief Executive Officer, bringing over 35 years of banking industry experience.\u000a- **Strategic Moves:** Continued expansion of commercial lending and wealth management services, with a focus on relationship banking.\u000a- **Funding and Acquisitions:** No recent public announcements of major funding rounds or acquisitions as of late 2024. The bank remains a subsidiary of BayCom Corp, which may influence future strategic initiatives.\u000a- **Regulatory Compliance:** Maintains high regulatory standards, with ongoing compliance with FDIC and Federal Reserve requirements.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **7. Company Culture, Values, and Mission**\u000a\u000a- **Mission:** To provide knowledgeable and professional service, helping clients manage cash flow, manage debt, and grow their businesses.\u000a- **Values:**\u000a  - Client-centric approach\u000a  - Integrity and transparency\u000a  - Community involvement\u000a  - Innovation and adaptability\u000a- **Culture:** Emphasizes relationship banking, where employees are encouraged to build strong, long-term relationships with clients. The bank fosters a collaborative environment, with a focus on continuous learning and professional development.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **8. Leadership Team and Organizational Structure**\u000a\u000a- **President and CEO:** Nick Dyer (over 35 years of banking industry experience)\u000a- **Founding Director and CEO:** George J. Guarini (visionary behind the bank’s founding in 2004)\u000a- **Founding Director:** Robert G. Laverne (anesthesiologist and property developer)\u000a- **Director:** Bhupen B. Amin (general counsel and COO of Lotus Hotels and Investments)\u000a\u000aThe bank’s organizational structure is designed to support relationship banking, with senior management actively involved in client interactions and strategic decision-making.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **9. Technology Stack and Innovations**\u000a\u000a- **Core Technologies:**\u000a  - JavaScript, HTML, reCAPTCHA (for web security)\u000a  - Online and mobile banking platforms\u000a  - Treasury management systems\u000a  - Secure transaction processing\u000a\u000a- **Innovations:**\u000a  - Advanced online banking solutions for businesses and individuals\u000a  - Integration of secure payment and transaction systems\u000a  - Focus on digital transformation to enhance customer experience\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### **10. Market Position, Growth Trajectory, and Industry Standing**\u000a\u000a- **Market Position:** United Business Bank is a well-regarded regional bank with a strong presence in California and Colorado. Its Bauer 5 Star rating underscores its financial stability and commitment to excellence.\u000a- **Growth Trajectory:** The bank has demonstrated steady growth in revenue and client base, driven by its focus on relationship banking and specialized services. Employee count has remained stable, indicating a focus on quality over rapid expansion.\u000a- **Industry Standing:** Recognized for its financial strength, regulatory compliance, and client-centric approach. The bank is a trusted partner for businesses and individuals seeking personalized banking solutions.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a**Sources:**\u000a- RocketReach\u000a- United Business Bank Official Website\u000a- The Business Journals\u000a- FDIC BankFind Suite\u000a- Datanyze\u000a- ZoomInfo\u000a\u000aThis comprehensive overview provides a detailed snapshot of United Business Bank as a business entity, highlighting its operations, market position, and strategic direction.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE ===\u000a\u000a### Sales Opportunity Analysis: Rick Pak, EVP & Chief Lending Officer, United Business Bank\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Industry Trends Affecting United Business Bank’s Sector**\u000a\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate (CRE) Market Volatility:** The CRE sector is experiencing a slowdown due to higher interest rates, remote work trends, and reduced demand for office space. Banks are seeing increased loan delinquencies and are tightening underwriting standards.\u000a- **Small Business Lending Growth:** Despite economic uncertainty, demand for SBA 7(a), SBA 504, USDA, and California SBLGP loans remains strong, especially among minority-owned and immigrant-owned businesses, which are a key segment for United Business Bank.\u000a- **Digital Banking Adoption:** Community and regional banks are investing in digital loan origination, e-signature platforms, and online customer portals to remain competitive with larger institutions.\u000a- **Consolidation and M&A Activity:** The banking sector is seeing increased merger and acquisition activity, with smaller banks seeking scale and efficiency. United Business Bank (now part of BayCom Corp) is a product of this trend.\u000a- **Focus on ESG and Community Lending:** Regulators and customers are pushing banks to demonstrate commitment to environmental, social, and governance (ESG) principles, including community reinvestment and support for underserved markets.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Common Pain Points for EVP Chief Lending Officer Roles**\u000a\u000a- **Loan Portfolio Quality:** Managing credit risk in a rising rate environment, especially with CRE and commercial loans, is a top concern.\u000a- **Regulatory Compliance:** Keeping up with evolving regulations (e.g., Basel III, CECL, CRA) and ensuring loan documentation and reporting are audit-ready.\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** Streamlining loan origination, underwriting, and servicing processes to reduce costs and turnaround times.\u000a- **Talent Retention:** Attracting and retaining experienced lending professionals in a competitive labor market.\u000a- **Customer Experience:** Balancing personalized service with digital transformation to meet customer expectations.\u000a- **Competitive Pricing:** Offering competitive rates while maintaining profitability and capital adequacy.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Regulatory or Compliance Challenges**\u000a\u000a- **Basel III and Capital Adequacy:** Ensuring sufficient capital ratios and liquidity to meet regulatory requirements.\u000a- **CECL Implementation:** Ongoing compliance with Current Expected Credit Loss (CECL) standards for loan loss provisioning.\u000a- **CRA and Community Lending:** Meeting Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) requirements, especially in diverse markets like California.\u000a- **Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and KYC:** Robust customer due diligence and transaction monitoring to prevent financial crime.\u000a- **Fair Lending and ECOA:** Ensuring lending practices are fair and non-discriminatory, with proper documentation and reporting.\u000a- **Cybersecurity and Data Privacy:** Protecting customer data and complying with state and federal privacy laws (e.g., CCPA, GLBA).\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 4. **Technology Trends Impacting Their Business**\u000a\u000a- **Digital Loan Origination Platforms:** Adoption of end-to-end digital loan origination, underwriting, and servicing solutions to improve efficiency and customer experience.\u000a- **AI and Machine Learning:** Use of AI for credit scoring, fraud detection, and portfolio risk management.\u000a- **eSignature and Remote Notarization:** Increasing use of e-signature platforms (e.g., DocuSign, Adobe Sign) and remote online notarization (RON) to streamline loan closings.\u000a- **Core Banking System Upgrades:** Migration to modern core banking platforms to support digital banking, real-time payments, and improved reporting.\u000a- **Data Analytics and Business Intelligence:** Leveraging data analytics for portfolio management, risk assessment, and strategic decision-making.\u000a- **Cloud Computing:** Adoption of cloud-based solutions for scalability, disaster recovery, and cost efficiency.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 5. **Economic Factors Affecting Decision-Making**\u000a\u000a- **Interest Rate Environment:** Higher rates increase funding costs and reduce loan demand, especially for CRE and commercial loans.\u000a- **Inflation and Cost Pressures:** Rising operational costs (e.g., salaries, technology, compliance) impact profitability and lending margins.\u000a- **Economic Uncertainty:** Recession fears and market volatility make businesses cautious about borrowing and investing.\u000a- **Regional Economic Conditions:** California’s economy, particularly in Silicon Valley and Southern California, is a key driver of loan demand and credit risk.\u000a- **Government Policy:** Federal and state government support programs (e.g., SBA, USDA, California SBLGP) influence lending activity and risk appetite.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 6. **Recent Company Challenges or Opportunities in the News**\u000a\u000a- **Core System Upgrade:** United Business Bank recently completed a core system upgrade to improve efficiency, support new digital offerings, and enhance customer experience (as of July 2020, with ongoing enhancements).\u000a- **Digital Transformation:** The bank has introduced e-signature capabilities and is expanding online loan services to meet customer demand for digital banking.\u000a- **M&A Integration:** As part of BayCom Corp, United Business Bank is integrating operations and systems with other acquired banks, which presents both challenges (integration complexity) and opportunities (scale, cross-selling).\u000a- **Focus on Community Lending:** The bank continues to emphasize support for small businesses, minority-owned businesses, and community development, aligning with CRA and ESG goals.\u000a- **ATM Modernization:** Replacement of ATMs with image-capable machines to improve cash handling and customer convenience.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 7. **Typical Buying Triggers for This Persona Type**\u000a\u000a- **Regulatory Changes:** New or updated regulations (e.g., CECL, AML, KYC) often trigger investments in compliance technology and consulting.\u000a- **Operational Inefficiencies:** Pain points in loan origination, underwriting, or servicing processes drive demand for automation and workflow solutions.\u000a- **Customer Experience Gaps:** Customer feedback or competitive pressure prompts investments in digital banking, e-signature, and customer service tools.\u000a- **Growth Initiatives:** Expansion into new markets, product lines, or customer segments requires scalable technology and operational support.\u000a- **M&A or Integration:** Bank mergers or acquisitions create opportunities for technology upgrades, process standardization, and integration services.\u000a- **Risk Management Needs:** Increased credit risk or portfolio volatility leads to demand for risk analytics, portfolio management, and credit scoring solutions.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 8. **Budget Cycles and Procurement Patterns in This Industry**\u000a\u000a- **Annual Budgeting:** Most banks set annual budgets in Q4 for the following year, with procurement decisions often made in Q1 and Q2.\u000a- **Capital Expenditures:** Major technology investments (e.g., core system upgrades, digital transformation) are typically planned and approved as part of the annual capital budget.\u000a- **Procurement Process:** Banks often have formal procurement processes, including RFPs, vendor evaluations, and board approvals for significant purchases.\u000a- **Vendor Relationships:** Long-term vendor relationships are common, with a preference for established, reputable providers with banking industry experience.\u000a- **Compliance and Security:** Procurement decisions are heavily influenced by compliance, security, and regulatory requirements.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 9. **Key Initiatives Companies Like United Business Bank Are Prioritizing**\u000a\u000a- **Digital Transformation:** Investing in digital loan origination, online banking, and customer self-service platforms.\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** Streamlining processes, reducing costs, and improving turnaround times for lending and servicing.\u000a- **Risk Management:** Enhancing credit risk, portfolio management, and compliance capabilities.\u000a- **Customer Experience:** Improving digital and in-person customer service, personalization, and omnichannel engagement.\u000a- **Community and ESG Lending:** Expanding support for small businesses, minority-owned businesses, and community development projects.\u000a- **M&A Integration:** Integrating operations, systems, and cultures following mergers and acquisitions.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 10. **Competitive Pressures They’re Facing**\u000a\u000a- **Large National Banks:** Competing with larger institutions that offer broader product suites, lower rates, and more advanced digital banking.\u000a- **Fintech Disruption:** Facing competition from fintech lenders and digital banks that offer faster, more convenient lending solutions.\u000a- **Regional and Community Banks:** Competing with other regional and community banks for market share, talent, and customer loyalty.\u000a- **Customer Expectations:** Meeting rising customer expectations for digital banking, personalized service, and fast loan approvals.\u000a- **Regulatory and Compliance Costs:** Managing the increasing burden of regulatory and compliance requirements, which can erode profitability and competitiveness.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Actionable Intelligence for Sales Engagement\u000a\u000a- **Focus on Digital Transformation:** Position your solution as a way to accelerate digital loan origination, improve customer experience, and streamline operations.\u000a- **Highlight Compliance and Risk Management:** Emphasize how your product or service helps with regulatory compliance, risk management, and audit readiness.\u000a- **Leverage Industry Trends:** Use data and insights on CRE, small business lending, and digital banking to demonstrate relevance and urgency.\u000a- **Address Operational Pain Points:** Offer solutions that reduce costs, improve efficiency, and enhance loan portfolio quality.\u000a- **Align with Strategic Initiatives:** Show how your offering supports United Business Bank’s priorities, such as digital transformation, community lending, and M&A integration.\u000a- **Timing and Budget Cycles:** Engage in Q4 and Q1 to align with annual budgeting and procurement cycles.\u000a- **Build Relationships:** Focus on building long-term relationships with key decision-makers, including Rick Pak and other members of the executive team.\u000a- **Differentiate from Competitors:** Highlight unique features, industry expertise, and proven results that set your solution apart from larger banks and fintech competitors.\u000a\u000aBy understanding these factors and tailoring your sales approach accordingly, you can effectively engage Rick Pak and United Business Bank to drive meaningful business opportunities.\u000a\u000a\u000a=== PERSONALITY ANALYSIS ===\u000a\u000a## Myers-Briggs (MBTI) Assessment\u000a\u000a**Inferred Type:** ENTJ  \u000a**Confidence:** Medium\u000a\u000a| Dimension | Preference | Evidence from Research |\u000a|-----------|------------|----------------------|\u000a| Energy | E - Extraversion | Rick Pak''s roles in executive leadership and strategic direction suggest a preference for engaging with others and leading teams. |\u000a| Information | N - Intuition | His focus on strategic growth, innovation, and driving organic loan growth indicates a forward-thinking and visionary approach. |\u000a| Decisions | T - Thinking | Emphasis on strategic initiatives, results, and operational management points to a logical, objective decision-making style. |\u000a| Structure | J - Judging | His management style is described as hands-on and strategic, with a focus on driving results, suggesting a preference for structure and planning. |\u000a\u000a**Work Style:** Likely approaches work with a strategic, results-oriented mindset, focusing on innovation and leadership. Prefers structured environments where he can lead teams towards achieving long-term goals.\u000a\u000a## DISC Profile Assessment\u000a\u000a**Primary Style:** D - Dominance  \u000a**Secondary Style:** C - Conscientiousness\u000a\u000a| Style | Estimated % | Evidence |\u000a|-------|-------------|----------|\u000a| D - Dominance | 40% | Evidence of direct, results-oriented behavior is seen in his strategic leadership roles and focus on driving loan growth and innovation. |\u000a| I - Influence | 20% | While he emphasizes collaboration, there is less evidence of a primary focus on people-oriented behavior. |\u000a| S - Steadiness | 10% | Limited evidence of patient, reliable behavior; more emphasis on strategic change and growth. |\u000a| C - Conscientiousness | 30% | His detailed focus on specific lending programs and structured management style indicates attention to detail and analytical behavior. |\u000a\u000a## Communication Playbook\u000a\u000a### ✅ DO: How to Engage This Person\u000a- Present clear, strategic goals and objectives.\u000a- Focus on results and how they align with long-term vision.\u000a- Be prepared with data and evidence to support proposals.\u000a\u000a### ❌ DON''T: What to Avoid\u000a- Avoid vague or unstructured communication.\u000a- Do not focus solely on personal stories or emotional appeals without data.\u000a- Avoid last-minute changes or lack of preparation.\u000a\u000a### 🎯 Best Opening Approach\u000aBegin with a concise overview of strategic opportunities or challenges, highlighting potential for growth and innovation. Use data to support your points and align with his focus on results and strategic direction.'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-04T18:38:14.367286','Rick','Pak',NULL,'','San Jose','California','',0,'21198337852',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
