INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4647,'Greg Richter','greg.richter@medalistpartners.com','+1212-493-4477','Medalist Partners',NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-03T17:41:01.909808','2025-12-03T17:41:01.909809',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','Chief Executive Officer','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.909808',0,NULL,unistr('\u000a=== PERSON RESEARCH: Greg Richter ===\u000a# Greg Richter: Chief Executive Officer at Medalist Partners\u000a\u000a## Current Role and Responsibilities\u000a\u000aGreg Richter serves as **Chief Executive Officer and Partner at Medalist Partners**, a $2 billion alternative credit manager headquartered in New York that specializes in asset-based private credit.[1][2] In addition to his CEO duties, Richter holds the position of **Co-Head of Structured Credit and Asset Finance** and serves as a **Co-Portfolio Manager** for the funds managed by Medalist.[2][3] He assumed these roles when he co-founded Medalist Partners on May 1, 2018, alongside Michael Ardisson and John Slonieski.[5]\u000a\u000a## Career History\u000a\u000aRichter''s career in finance spans nearly four decades, beginning in 1985 during the formative years of the structured credit market.[4] His professional trajectory demonstrates deep expertise in asset-based finance and securitized products:\u000a\u000a**Prudential Securities (approximately 1985-2000)**\u000aRichter began his career at Prudential Securities in a middle-office role that bridged operations and the mortgage trading desk.[4] This early exposure provided him with foundational knowledge in mortgage funding, allocations, and trading. He progressed rapidly through the organization, eventually becoming a **Managing Director** and **Head of Trading and Syndicate for all structured products**.[2] During his tenure at Prudential, which lasted approximately 15 years, Richter led all structured credit trading, including CMBS, RMBS, ABS, and CDOs.[4] His team achieved a significant milestone by creating and selling the first ABS CDOs, which subsequently became a major market segment.[4]\u000a\u000a**Credit Suisse (2000-2008)**\u000aRichter joined Credit Suisse in 2000, where he initially oversaw **trading and syndication for securitized products**.[4] Under his leadership, the firm packaged and sold $100-200 billion of bonds annually.[4] His responsibilities expanded significantly over his tenure. He became **Head of Credit Suisse''s Specialty Finance Group**, based in New York, where he was responsible for the combined Global Asset Finance Capital Markets and Specialty Finance Banking Groups.[2] He also served as a **member of Credit Suisse''s Fixed Income Operating Committee**.[2] Additionally, Richter headed the **Asset Backed Securities Home Equity (ABSHE) shelf**, which bought and packaged mortgage loans.[2] His role encompassed oversight of all securitization activity in the U.S., Europe, Australia, and Emerging Markets.[2]\u000a\u000a**Candlewood Investment Group (2008-2018)**\u000aFollowing the 2008 financial crisis, Richter transitioned to asset management. He co-founded and led the **Structured Credit and Asset Finance business** within Candlewood Investment Group, LP, serving as a Partner and Director.[2]\u000a\u000a**Medalist Partners (2018-Present)**\u000aRichter co-founded Medalist Partners in May 2018, where he has served as CEO and Partner for over seven years.[5] The firm was established to provide funding in specialty finance, particularly where traditional banks have retreated.[4]\u000a\u000a## Education and Credentials\u000a\u000aRichter holds a **Bachelor of Arts degree in Economics from Colgate University**.[2]\u000a\u000a## Professional Achievements and Recognition\u000a\u000aRichter''s career has been marked by several significant accomplishments:\u000a\u000a- **Pioneer in ABS CDO Market**: His team at Prudential created and sold the first ABS CDOs, establishing a market that grew substantially in subsequent years.[4]\u000a- **Large-Scale Securitization Operations**: At Credit Suisse, he oversaw the packaging and sale of $100-200 billion in bonds annually across multiple markets and product types.[4]\u000a- **2025 Icon Recognition**: Richter was recognized as a 2025 Icon by ABF Journal, acknowledging his nearly four decades at the center of innovation in asset-based finance.[4]\u000a- **Firm Leadership**: He successfully established Medalist Partners as a $2 billion alternative credit manager focused on asset-based private credit, managing capital for institutional investors including pensions, endowments, foundations, family offices, and high-net-worth individuals.[1][5]\u000a\u000a## Speaking Engagements and Media Appearances\u000a\u000aRichter has been active in the speaking circuit. He has appeared as a speaker and organizer at industry conferences, including events hosted by the Council of Federal Advisors of New York (CFANY).[1] His media presence includes recognition in industry publications such as ABF Journal, where he has shared insights on the evolution of structured credit markets and the specialty finance sector.[4]\u000a\u000a## Professional Associations and Board Positions\u000a\u000aIn addition to his CEO role at Medalist Partners, Richter serves as a **Director/Board Member** at Revolution Microelectronics (America), Inc., a manufacturer and distributor of aerospace-grade horticultural lighting and controls products headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia.[5]\u000a\u000a## Professional Philosophy\u000a\u000aRichter emphasizes leadership through example and interpersonal relationships. He has stated: "Always treat people with respect and thoughtfulness. This is a people business. Never underestimate the importance of a handshake and good conversation."[4] His vision for Medalist Partners is to establish it as a trusted, innovative firm that addresses market needs in specialty finance where traditional banking has contracted.[4]\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH: Medalist Partners ===\u000a# Medalist Partners: Comprehensive Business Research\u000a\u000a## Company Overview and Fundamentals\u000a\u000aMedalist Partners, LP is a private institutional investment management firm headquartered at 777 Third Avenue, Suite 1402, New York City, New York[1][2]. The firm operates as an alternative investment management company specializing in credit-focused investment strategies[3][4].\u000a\u000aThe company manages capital with an estimated annual revenue of approximately $9.8 million[6]. Medalist Partners serves a sophisticated institutional investor base, managing capital primarily on behalf of pensions, endowments, foundations, family offices, and high net worth individuals[1].\u000a\u000a## Business Model and Service Offerings\u000a\u000aMedalist Partners operates through a diversified investment vehicle structure designed to accommodate different investor preferences and regulatory requirements. The firm offers solutions through three primary channels[1]:\u000a\u000a- Private funds\u000a- Registered funds (accessible through www.medalistpartnersfunds.com)\u000a- Separately managed accounts\u000a\u000aThe company''s investment strategies focus specifically on credit opportunities, with core competencies in three areas[3]:\u000a\u000a- Asset-Based Private Credit\u000a- Structured Credit\u000a- Specialty vehicles\u000a\u000aThis positioning places Medalist Partners in the alternative credit investment space, where it provides institutional-grade investment solutions in non-traditional lending and structured finance markets.\u000a\u000a## Target Markets and Customer Segments\u000a\u000aMedalist Partners'' target market consists exclusively of institutional and high-net-worth investors[1]. The firm''s customer segments include:\u000a\u000a- Pension funds\u000a- University endowments\u000a- Charitable foundations\u000a- Family offices\u000a- High net worth individuals\u000a\u000aThis institutional focus reflects a business model centered on managing substantial capital pools for sophisticated investors seeking alternative credit exposure.\u000a\u000a## Competitive Landscape\u000a\u000aThe search results do not provide specific information about Medalist Partners'' key competitors or detailed competitive positioning. However, the firm operates within the broader alternative credit and structured finance investment management industry, competing against other specialized credit managers serving institutional investors.\u000a\u000a## Recent Developments, Funding, and Strategic Initiatives\u000a\u000aThe search results do not contain information regarding recent news, funding rounds, acquisitions, or strategic moves by Medalist Partners. No specific dates or recent developments are documented in the available sources.\u000a\u000a## Company Culture and Values\u000a\u000aThe search results do not provide explicit information about Medalist Partners'' stated company culture, values, or organizational philosophy.\u000a\u000a## Leadership Team and Organizational Structure\u000a\u000aThe search results do not contain details about Medalist Partners'' leadership team, executive officers, or organizational structure.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a**Note on Data Limitations**: The available search results provide foundational information about Medalist Partners'' core business focus and basic operational details, but lack comprehensive coverage of recent developments, leadership information, competitive analysis, and strategic initiatives. For more detailed research on these areas, additional sources beyond the current search results would be necessary.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE ===\u000a# Sales Intelligence Analysis: Greg Richter, CEO at Medalist Partners\u000a\u000a## Company Overview & Market Position\u000a\u000aMedalist Partners is an alternative investment management firm based in New York specializing in structured credit and asset finance[3]. The firm focuses on credit opportunities across multiple asset classes including commercial real estate, residential real estate, and asset-based private credit[2]. This positions them within the competitive alternative investment management sector where institutional capital allocation and credit market expertise are critical differentiators.\u000a\u000a## Industry Trends Affecting Medalist Partners\u000a\u000a**Structured Credit Market Expansion**\u000a\u000aThe structured credit and alternative asset finance sectors have experienced significant growth as institutional investors seek yield diversification beyond traditional fixed income. This trend accelerates demand for sophisticated credit analysis capabilities and portfolio management platforms that can handle complex securitized products across multiple asset classes.\u000a\u000a**Consolidation and Scale Pressures**\u000a\u000aThe alternative investment management industry continues to consolidate, with larger firms acquiring specialized boutiques. Medalist Partners'' focus on niche credit opportunities positions them in a competitive landscape where differentiation through technology, data analytics, and operational efficiency becomes increasingly important for maintaining market share and attracting institutional capital.\u000a\u000a**Regulatory Capital Requirements**\u000a\u000aAs an investment manager handling structured credit products, Medalist Partners operates under evolving regulatory frameworks. The post-2008 financial crisis regulatory environment continues to impose compliance burdens, particularly around risk management, reporting, and capital adequacy standards for firms managing securitized assets.\u000a\u000a## Pain Points for CEO Leadership in This Sector\u000a\u000a**Portfolio Performance Consistency**\u000a\u000aAccording to Morningstar data, Medalist Partners has experienced high portfolio management turnover over the past five years, with the firm''s average five-year Morningstar Rating at 2.6 stars[4]. This suggests performance volatility and team stability challenges—critical concerns for a CEO managing institutional investor expectations and capital retention.\u000a\u000a**Talent Retention and Continuity**\u000a\u000aThe structured credit and asset finance sectors require highly specialized expertise. High portfolio management turnover indicates difficulty retaining experienced professionals, which directly impacts investment decision quality and client confidence. CEOs in this space face constant pressure to build and maintain teams with deep market knowledge across multiple asset classes.\u000a\u000a**Competitive Differentiation**\u000a\u000aWith numerous firms competing in structured credit, CEOs must continuously demonstrate unique investment theses, superior risk-adjusted returns, and operational advantages. The "Below Average Parent Pillar rating" from Morningstar suggests Medalist Partners faces competitive positioning challenges[4].\u000a\u000a**Investor Relations and Capital Raising**\u000a\u000aAlternative investment managers depend on continuous capital inflows. CEOs must manage investor communications, demonstrate consistent performance, and navigate increasingly sophisticated investor due diligence processes. The need for robust investor relations infrastructure and reporting capabilities is paramount.\u000a\u000a## Regulatory and Compliance Challenges\u000a\u000a**Securitization Oversight**\u000a\u000aMedalist Partners'' involvement in structured credit and securitized products places them under SEC and potentially FINRA oversight. The firm must maintain compliance with:\u000a- Investment Advisers Act regulations\u000a- Form ADV filing requirements\u000a- Valuation and pricing standards for illiquid securities\u000a- Conflict of interest disclosures\u000a\u000a**Risk Management Documentation**\u000a\u000aRegulatory expectations around risk management frameworks, stress testing, and scenario analysis have intensified. Firms managing credit-sensitive portfolios must demonstrate sophisticated risk measurement and mitigation capabilities.\u000a\u000a**Reporting and Transparency**\u000a\u000aInstitutional investors increasingly demand detailed reporting on portfolio composition, credit quality, liquidity profiles, and performance attribution. Compliance with these demands requires robust data infrastructure and reporting systems.\u000a\u000a## Technology Trends Impacting the Business\u000a\u000a**Data Analytics and AI-Driven Credit Analysis**\u000a\u000aThe structured credit sector is increasingly adopting machine learning and artificial intelligence for credit underwriting, portfolio monitoring, and risk prediction. Firms that leverage advanced analytics gain competitive advantages in identifying mispriced credit opportunities and managing portfolio risk.\u000a\u000a**Portfolio Management Systems**\u000a\u000aModern portfolio management platforms offering real-time analytics, scenario modeling, and integrated risk reporting are becoming table stakes. Legacy systems create operational friction and limit analytical capabilities.\u000a\u000a**Cybersecurity and Data Protection**\u000a\u000aAs a manager of institutional capital, Medalist Partners faces heightened cybersecurity requirements. Institutional investors demand robust data protection, business continuity planning, and cyber insurance verification.\u000a\u000a**Investor Portal and Reporting Automation**\u000a\u000aInstitutional investors expect self-service portals, automated reporting, and real-time performance dashboards. Technology investments in investor-facing platforms directly impact capital retention and fundraising efficiency.\u000a\u000a## Economic Factors Affecting Decision-Making\u000a\u000a**Interest Rate Environment**\u000a\u000aStructured credit valuations and yields are highly sensitive to interest rate movements. In a higher-for-longer rate environment, credit spreads compress, affecting portfolio returns and investor demand for yield-focused strategies. CEOs must navigate asset allocation decisions based on rate forecasts.\u000a\u000a**Credit Cycle Positioning**\u000a\u000aEconomic slowdown concerns affect credit quality across asset classes. CEOs managing credit-focused strategies must make tactical decisions about portfolio positioning, duration, and credit quality as economic indicators shift.\u000a\u000a**Institutional Capital Flows**\u000a\u000aPension funds, endowments, and insurance companies adjust alternative asset allocations based on economic outlook and liability matching needs. Economic uncertainty can trigger capital redemptions or reduced new commitments, directly impacting revenue and AUM growth.\u000a\u000a**Liquidity Conditions**\u000a\u000aStructured credit markets can experience liquidity stress during market dislocations. CEOs must ensure adequate liquidity buffers and stress-test portfolio resilience against various market scenarios.\u000a\u000a## Typical Buying Triggers for CEO Personas\u000a\u000a**Performance Underperformance**\u000a\u000aWhen risk-adjusted returns lag peer benchmarks or investor expectations, CEOs actively seek solutions to improve performance. Morningstar''s 2.6-star average rating suggests this may be relevant for Medalist Partners.\u000a\u000a**Operational Inefficiency**\u000a\u000aHigh portfolio management turnover and operational friction create urgency around talent retention, workflow optimization, and systems modernization. CEOs facing these challenges seek solutions that improve operational leverage.\u000a\u000a**Investor Redemption Pressure**\u000a\u000aWhen AUM declines or redemption requests increase, CEOs prioritize investor relations improvements, performance enhancement, and cost optimization initiatives.\u000a\u000a**Competitive Positioning Threats**\u000a\u000aMarket share loss to competitors or difficulty attracting institutional capital triggers strategic initiatives around differentiation, technology investment, and market positioning.\u000a\u000a**Regulatory or Compliance Issues**\u000a\u000aRegulatory findings, audit deficiencies, or compliance gaps create urgent need for remediation solutions and enhanced compliance infrastructure.\u000a\u000a**Technology Modernization Needs**\u000a\u000aAging systems, reporting delays, or analytical limitations create business cases for technology platform upgrades and digital transformation initiatives.\u000a\u000a**Talent Acquisition and Retention**\u000a\u000aDifficulty recruiting and retaining specialized credit professionals drives investment in HR technology, compensation optimization, and workplace culture initiatives.\u000a\u000a## Recommended Sales Approach\u000a\u000aTarget Greg Richter with solutions addressing:\u000a- Portfolio performance analytics and risk management tools\u000a- Operational efficiency and workflow automation\u000a- Investor relations and reporting platforms\u000a- Talent management and retention technology\u000a- Compliance and regulatory reporting infrastructure\u000a- Credit analysis and underwriting technology\u000a\u000aPosition solutions around competitive differentiation, operational leverage, and institutional investor satisfaction—the core concerns for alternative investment management CEOs in the structured credit space.\u000a'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-04T02:03:32.345464','Greg','Richter',NULL,'','New York','New York','',0,'59457889379',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
