INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4715,'Nur Nadir','n@sopuru.com','(323) 627-1177','American Business Bank - PLP SBA Lender',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/nurnadir',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-03T17:41:01.917765','2025-12-03T17:41:01.917766',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','Vice President/SBA Business Development - (323) 627 - 1177 - nnadir@americanbb.bank - ABB/SBA PLP Lender','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.917764',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,unistr('=== PERSON RESEARCH: Nur Nadir ===\u000a\u000a# Research Analysis: Nur Nadir\u000a\u000aBased on the available search results, I can provide the following information about Nur Nadir:\u000a\u000a## Current Role and Position\u000a\u000aNur Nadir currently serves as **Vice President, SBA Division** at American Business Bank[1][3]. His specific focus is on SBA (Small Business Administration) Business Development[1][2]. He is based in Los Angeles, California, United States[1][4].\u000a\u000a**Contact Information:**\u000a- Office: 310-741-0838[3]\u000a- Mobile: 323-627-1177[1][3]\u000a- Email: nnadir@americanbb.bank[1][3]\u000a\u000a## Employer Information\u000a\u000aNur Nadir works at **American Business Bank**, which is a $3.2 billion commercial bank[2]. The bank trades on the OTCQX exchange under the symbol AMBZ[2]. American Business Bank maintains its headquarters in Los Angeles, CA 90071[5], with multiple regional loan production offices throughout Southern California, including locations in Anaheim and Woodland Hills[5].\u000a\u000a## Professional Context\u000a\u000aAs part of the SBA Division at American Business Bank, Nur Nadir operates within the bank''s specialized lending unit focused on Small Business Administration loans. The bank''s SBA Loan Production Office is located at 2400 E. Katella Ave., Suite 700, Anaheim, California 92806[5].\u000a\u000a## Limitations of Available Information\u000a\u000aUnfortunately, the search results do not contain comprehensive information regarding:\u000a- Complete career history with specific dates and previous positions\u000a- Educational background, degrees, or professional credentials\u000a- Professional achievements, awards, or recognition\u000a- LinkedIn activity, posts, or thought leadership content\u000a- Speaking engagements, podcasts, or media appearances beyond conference presentations\u000a- Board positions or other professional affiliations\u000a- Management style or professional philosophy\u000a- Specific notable deals or projects\u000a\u000aThe search results indicate that Nur Nadir has presented at the 2020 CABB (California Association of Business Banks) Annual Conference[2], and he maintains a LinkedIn profile[3], but detailed information from these sources is not available in the provided search results.\u000a\u000aTo obtain comprehensive information on the remaining points, direct contact through his provided email or phone number, or access to his complete LinkedIn profile would be necessary.\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH: American Business Bank - PLP SBA Lender ===\u000a\u000a### 1. Company Overview\u000a\u000a**Company Name:** American Business Bank (ABB)  \u000a**Ticker Symbol:** AMBZ (OTCQX)  \u000a**Founded:** 1998  \u000a**Headquarters:** 400 South Hope Street, Suite 300, Los Angeles, CA 90071  \u000a**Total Assets:** $3.3 billion (as of latest public reporting)  \u000a**Employee Count:** Not publicly disclosed, but estimated to be in the range of 200–300 based on branch and loan production office footprint and executive team size.  \u000a**Revenue:** Not publicly disclosed in SEC filings or press releases; typical for OTC-listed banks, revenue is derived from net interest income, fees, and SBA lending activities.  \u000a**Ownership Structure:** Publicly traded on OTCQX, not listed on major exchanges.  \u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 2. Business Model and How They Make Money\u000a\u000aAmerican Business Bank operates as a commercial bank focused on small and mid-sized businesses, particularly in California. The bank’s primary revenue streams include:\u000a\u000a- **Net Interest Income:** Earned from loans (commercial, SBA, asset-based, real estate) and deposits.\u000a- **SBA Lending Fees:** As an SBA Preferred Lender, ABB earns fees from originating and servicing SBA 7(a) and 504 loans, including guarantee fees and servicing income.\u000a- **Asset-Based Lending:** Revenue from interest and fees on loans secured by business assets (accounts receivable, inventory, equipment).\u000a- **International Banking Services:** Fees from foreign exchange, trade finance, and cross-border transactions.\u000a- **Consulting & Referral Services:** Advisory and referral fees for business consulting and connecting clients with professional services.\u000a- **Deposit Services:** Interest spread from commercial and business checking/savings accounts.\u000a\u000aThe bank’s business model emphasizes relationship banking, where long-term client relationships drive recurring revenue and cross-selling opportunities.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 3. Products, Services, and Offerings\u000a\u000a**Core Products & Services:**\u000a\u000a- **SBA Lending:**\u000a  - SBA 7(a) Loans: General-purpose loans up to $5 million, with expedited approval as a Preferred Lender.\u000a  - SBA 504 Loans: Fixed-asset financing (real estate, equipment) up to $5.5 million, with down payments as low as 10% for owner-occupied real estate.\u000a  - SBA Preferred Lender Program (PLP): Streamlined approval process for SBA loans, reducing processing time for borrowers.\u000a\u000a- **Asset-Based Lending:**\u000a  - Loans secured by accounts receivable, inventory, and equipment for distributors, manufacturers, wholesalers, and service companies.\u000a  - Designed for businesses with rapid growth, seasonal sales, acquisitions, or restructuring needs.\u000a\u000a- **Commercial Banking:**\u000a  - Business checking and savings accounts.\u000a  - Commercial real estate loans.\u000a  - Lines of credit.\u000a\u000a- **International Banking:**\u000a  - Foreign exchange services.\u000a  - Trade finance and letters of credit.\u000a  - Cross-border transaction support.\u000a\u000a- **Consulting & Referral Services:**\u000a  - Business advisory and mentorship.\u000a  - Referrals to legal, accounting, and other professional services.\u000a\u000a- **Loan Production Offices (LPOs):**\u000a  - Multiple LPOs in California (Los Angeles, Anaheim, Woodland Hills, Torrance, Riverside) to facilitate local lending and client engagement.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 4. Target Markets and Customer Segments\u000a\u000a**Primary Target Markets:**\u000a- Small and mid-sized businesses (SMBs) in California, with a focus on entrepreneurs and business owners.\u000a- Businesses seeking SBA-guaranteed loans for expansion, real estate, or equipment.\u000a- Companies requiring asset-based lending for growth or restructuring.\u000a- Businesses with international operations or trade needs.\u000a\u000a**Customer Segments:**\u000a- Entrepreneurs and startups.\u000a- Established SMBs in manufacturing, distribution, wholesale, and service industries.\u000a- Real estate investors and developers.\u000a- Businesses with cross-border operations.\u000a\u000a**Geographic Focus:**\u000a- Primarily California, with some consideration for out-of-state applicants on a case-by-case basis.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 5. Competitive Landscape\u000a\u000a**Key Competitors:**\u000a- **City National Bank:** Major commercial bank in California with strong SMB focus.\u000a- **Pacific Western Bank:** Regional bank with significant SBA lending activity.\u000a- **Bank of the West:** Large regional bank with extensive commercial and SBA lending.\u000a- **Community banks and credit unions:** Local institutions offering similar services.\u000a\u000a**Differentiation:**\u000a- **SBA Preferred Lender Status:** Faster loan approvals and streamlined processes for SBA loans.\u000a- **Relationship-Focused Banking:** Emphasis on long-term partnerships and personalized service.\u000a- **Entrepreneurial Focus:** Founded by entrepreneurs, for entrepreneurs, with a deep understanding of SMB needs.\u000a- **Asset-Based Lending Expertise:** Specialized solutions for businesses with unique financing needs.\u000a- **Community Engagement:** Active participation in local business initiatives and community development.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 6. Recent News\u000a\u000a**Recent Developments:**\u000a- **September 2, 2025:** Opened a new Loan Production Office (LPO) in Downtown Riverside, expanding its footprint in Southern California.\u000a- **Ongoing:** Continued focus on SBA lending and asset-based lending, with regular updates on loan production and community engagement.\u000a\u000a**Funding, Acquisitions, Leadership Changes:**\u000a- No recent acquisitions or major funding rounds reported.\u000a- Leadership team remains stable, with Leon Blankstein as CEO and Director.\u000a\u000a**Strategic Moves:**\u000a- Expansion of LPOs to increase local presence and client engagement.\u000a- Continued investment in SBA lending and asset-based lending capabilities.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 7. Company Culture, Values, and Mission\u000a\u000a**Mission:** To create a financial institution “for entrepreneurs, by entrepreneurs,” fostering long-term partnerships and supporting business growth.\u000a\u000a**Core Values:**\u000a- **Trust:** Every relationship and decision is built on trust.\u000a- **Relationship Banking:** Focus on building honest, long-term relationships rather than transactional interactions.\u000a- **Community Engagement:** Active participation in local business initiatives and community development.\u000a- **Integrity:** Never promise what you can’t deliver.\u000a\u000a**Culture:**\u000a- Emphasis on collaboration, mentorship, and personalized service.\u000a- Employees are seen as the greatest asset, with a focus on protecting clients’ interests and driving business success.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 8. Leadership Team and Organizational Structure\u000a\u000a**Executive Leadership:**\u000a- **Leon Blankstein:** CEO and Director\u000a- **Philip C. Feghali:** President\u000a- **Karen Schoenbaum:** Executive Vice President, Chief Financial Officer\u000a- **Jeffrey Munson:** Executive Vice President, Chief Credit Officer\u000a- **David A. Wolf:** Executive Vice President\u000a- **Larry Pyle:** Senior Vice President, Deputy Chief Credit Officer\u000a- **Gary Allen Cook:** Regional Executive Vice President, Real Estate Manager\u000a- **David Plourde:** Regional Executive Vice President\u000a- **Stephen Pihl:** Regional Executive Vice President, SBA Manager\u000a- **Daniel Lykken:** Regional Executive Vice President\u000a\u000a**Organizational Structure:**\u000a- Centralized headquarters in Los Angeles with regional loan production offices.\u000a- Executive leadership oversees strategic direction, while regional executives manage local operations and client relationships.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 9. Technology Stack and Innovations\u000a\u000a**Technology Stack:**\u000a- Core banking platform (not publicly disclosed, but typical for regional banks: FIS, Jack Henry, or similar).\u000a- Online banking and mobile banking platforms for business clients.\u000a- SBA loan origination and servicing systems.\u000a- CRM and relationship management tools.\u000a\u000a**Innovations:**\u000a- Streamlined SBA loan approval process as a Preferred Lender.\u000a- Digital banking solutions for business clients.\u000a- Integration of consulting and referral services into client relationship management.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 10. Market Position, Growth Trajectory, and Industry Standing\u000a\u000a**Market Position:**\u000a- Leading regional commercial bank in California, with a strong focus on SMBs and SBA lending.\u000a- Recognized as an SBA Preferred Lender, enhancing its reputation and competitive advantage.\u000a\u000a**Growth Trajectory:**\u000a- Steady growth in assets and loan production, with recent expansion of LPOs.\u000a- Increasing focus on asset-based lending and international banking services.\u000a\u000a**Industry Standing:**\u000a- Well-regarded for relationship banking and community engagement.\u000a- Active participant in SBA lending, with a reputation for fast and reliable service.\u000a- Positioned as a trusted partner for entrepreneurs and SMBs in California.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Summary\u000a\u000aAmerican Business Bank is a California-based commercial bank founded in 1998, with $3.3 billion in assets and a strong focus on SMBs, SBA lending, and asset-based lending. The bank operates as an SBA Preferred Lender, offering streamlined loan approvals and personalized service. Its leadership team is stable, with a focus on relationship banking and community engagement. Recent strategic moves include the opening of new LPOs and continued investment in SBA and asset-based lending. The bank is well-positioned in the California market, with a reputation for trust, integrity, and entrepreneurial focus.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE ===\u000a\u000a### Sales Opportunity Analysis: Nur Nadir, Vice President/SBA Business Development – American Business Bank (ABB), PLP SBA Lender\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Industry Trends Affecting American Business Bank – PLP SBA Lender’s Sector (2025)**\u000a\u000a- **Record SBA 7(a) Lending Volume:** SBA 7(a) lending in 2025 is at near-record highs, with Q2 FY2025 (Jan–Mar 2025) seeing over $10 billion in approvals—the second-highest quarter in program history. This surge is driven by small-dollar loans (under $150,000), which now account for more than half of all 7(a) approvals. The trend reflects both strong entrepreneurial activity and cautious borrowing behavior among small businesses.\u000a- **Shift Toward Small-Dollar Loans:** Over 80% of 7(a) loans in early FY2025 were under $500,000. This indicates a growing demand for working capital and smaller expansion loans, rather than large real estate or acquisition financing.\u000a- **Increased Regulatory Scrutiny:** The SBA has implemented stricter underwriting standards in 2025, including mandatory delegated authority for PLP lenders, elimination of certain borrower-friendly provisions, and a moratorium on the expansion of the Community Advantage SBLC program due to high default rates.\u000a- **SBA Program Overhauls:** The SBA has overhauled the Community Advantage SBLC program, requiring higher capital reserves and stricter financial stability standards for mission-based lenders, and has suspended new licenses for this program.\u000a- **Technology Adoption:** PLP lenders are increasingly investing in digital loan origination platforms, automated underwriting, and compliance management systems to handle the increased volume and complexity of SBA lending.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Common Pain Points for Vice President/SBA Business Development Roles**\u000a\u000a- **Balancing Growth and Compliance:** VP/SBA Business Development must drive loan volume and market share while ensuring strict adherence to new SBA regulations and internal risk management policies.\u000a- **Managing Increased Operational Complexity:** The shift to mandatory delegated authority means PLP lenders must handle more underwriting and compliance internally, increasing the workload and risk exposure.\u000a- **Meeting Small-Business Demand:** With rising demand for small-dollar loans, VPs must ensure their teams can efficiently process a higher volume of smaller, more frequent applications.\u000a- **Maintaining Approval Rates:** Stricter SBA standards have led to lower approval rates, making it harder to meet sales targets and client expectations.\u000a- **Staying Competitive:** The SBA lending market is highly competitive, with top lenders like Live Oak Bank, Newtek Bank, and Northeast Bank dominating by volume and number of loans.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Regulatory or Compliance Challenges**\u000a\u000a- **Stricter Underwriting Requirements:** The SBA’s 2025 policy changes require PLP lenders to adhere to more rigorous underwriting standards, including enhanced documentation and risk assessment.\u000a- **Mandatory Delegated Authority:** PLP lenders must now process all eligible loans under delegated authority, increasing their compliance responsibility and potential liability.\u000a- **Prohibition on Merchant Cash Advance Refinancing:** SBA loans can no longer refinance merchant cash advances, closing a popular avenue for small businesses and reducing potential loan volume.\u000a- **Capital Reserve Requirements:** For Community Advantage SBLC lenders, the SBA has mandated higher capital reserves, which may impact liquidity and lending capacity.\u000a- **Ongoing Monitoring and Reporting:** PLP lenders must maintain robust internal controls and reporting systems to comply with SBA and FDIC requirements.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 4. **Technology Trends Impacting Their Business**\u000a\u000a- **Digital Loan Origination Platforms:** PLP lenders are adopting digital platforms to streamline the application, underwriting, and approval processes, reducing turnaround times and improving customer experience.\u000a- **Automated Underwriting and Compliance Tools:** Advanced software solutions are being used to automate underwriting decisions and ensure compliance with SBA regulations.\u000a- **Data Analytics and Risk Management:** Lenders are leveraging data analytics to identify high-risk borrowers, optimize loan portfolios, and improve decision-making.\u000a- **Integration with SBA Systems:** Seamless integration with SBA’s E-Tran and other digital systems is critical for efficient loan processing and compliance.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 5. **Economic Factors Affecting Their Decision-Making**\u000a\u000a- **Interest Rate Environment:** The current interest rate environment impacts both borrower demand and lender profitability. Higher rates may reduce demand for certain types of loans but increase the value of SBA-guaranteed paper in the secondary market.\u000a- **Economic Uncertainty:** Ongoing economic uncertainty, including inflation and potential recession risks, affects small-business confidence and borrowing behavior.\u000a- **Sector-Specific Challenges:** Certain industries, such as transportation and manufacturing, face unique financing challenges, which can impact loan demand and risk profiles.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 6. **Recent Company Challenges or Opportunities in the News**\u000a\u000a- **Increased Demand for Small-Dollar Loans:** The surge in small-dollar SBA loans presents both an opportunity for growth and a challenge in terms of operational efficiency and risk management.\u000a- **Regulatory Overhauls:** The SBA’s overhaul of the Community Advantage SBLC program and stricter underwriting standards have created both compliance challenges and opportunities for well-prepared institutions to gain market share.\u000a- **Technology Investments:** Leading PLP lenders are investing heavily in technology to improve loan processing, compliance, and customer service.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 7. **Typical Buying Triggers for This Persona Type**\u000a\u000a- **Need for Compliance Solutions:** VP/SBA Business Development is likely to seek solutions that help ensure compliance with new SBA regulations, such as automated underwriting and compliance management systems.\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** Solutions that streamline loan origination, underwriting, and servicing processes are highly valued, especially in the context of increased loan volume and complexity.\u000a- **Risk Management:** Tools and services that enhance risk assessment and portfolio management are in demand.\u000a- **Customer Experience:** Solutions that improve the borrower experience, such as digital application platforms and faster approval times, are attractive.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 8. **Budget Cycles and Procurement Patterns in This Industry**\u000a\u000a- **Annual Budget Cycles:** Most banks and financial institutions operate on annual budget cycles, with procurement decisions typically made in the first half of the year.\u000a- **Procurement Patterns:** Procurement is often driven by regulatory requirements, operational needs, and strategic initiatives. Vendors with proven compliance and efficiency solutions are preferred.\u000a- **Approval Processes:** Procurement decisions may involve multiple stakeholders, including compliance, risk management, and IT departments.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 9. **Key Initiatives Companies Like American Business Bank – PLP SBA Lender Are Prioritizing**\u000a\u000a- **Compliance and Risk Management:** Ensuring strict adherence to SBA regulations and internal risk management policies.\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** Streamlining loan origination, underwriting, and servicing processes to handle increased volume and complexity.\u000a- **Technology Adoption:** Investing in digital platforms, automated underwriting, and compliance management systems.\u000a- **Customer Experience:** Enhancing the borrower experience through digital solutions and faster approval times.\u000a- **Market Expansion:** Exploring new markets and borrower segments, particularly in underserved communities.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 10. **Competitive Pressures They’re Facing**\u000a\u000a- **Market Leaders:** Top lenders like Live Oak Bank, Newtek Bank, and Northeast Bank dominate by volume and number of loans, setting high standards for efficiency and service.\u000a- **Regulatory Compliance:** The need to comply with stricter SBA regulations creates a competitive advantage for lenders with robust compliance and risk management systems.\u000a- **Technology Adoption:** Lenders that invest in advanced technology solutions are better positioned to handle increased volume and complexity, and to provide superior customer service.\u000a- **Customer Expectations:** Small businesses expect fast, efficient, and transparent loan processes, putting pressure on lenders to innovate and improve their offerings.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Actionable Intelligence for Sales Engagement\u000a\u000a- **Focus on Compliance and Efficiency:** Highlight solutions that help ensure compliance with SBA regulations and streamline loan origination and underwriting processes.\u000a- **Emphasize Risk Management:** Offer tools and services that enhance risk assessment and portfolio management.\u000a- **Leverage Technology:** Showcase digital platforms and automated solutions that improve operational efficiency and customer experience.\u000a- **Address Small-Dollar Loan Demand:** Provide solutions tailored to the growing demand for small-dollar loans, such as streamlined application processes and automated underwriting.\u000a- **Timing:** Engage during the first half of the year when budget cycles are active and procurement decisions are being made.\u000a- **Stakeholder Engagement:** Involve compliance, risk management, and IT departments in the sales process to address their specific needs and concerns.\u000a\u000aBy addressing these key areas, you can position your solutions as essential for American Business Bank’s success in the evolving SBA lending landscape.\u000a\u000a\u000a=== PERSONALITY ANALYSIS ===\u000a\u000a## Myers-Briggs (MBTI) Assessment\u000a\u000a**Inferred Type:** ESTJ  \u000a**Confidence:** Medium\u000a\u000a| Dimension | Preference | Evidence from Research |\u000a|-----------|------------|----------------------|\u000a| Energy | E - Extraversion | Nur Nadir''s role as Vice President in Business Development suggests a focus on external engagement and networking, typical of extraverts. |\u000a| Information | S - Sensing | The emphasis on SBA lending and specific financial products indicates a detail-oriented approach, characteristic of sensing types. |\u000a| Decisions | T - Thinking | The focus on business development and lending suggests a logical, objective decision-making style. |\u000a| Structure | J - Judging | The structured nature of banking and lending, along with the role in business development, implies a preference for planning and organization. |\u000a\u000a**Work Style:** Likely approaches work with a focus on efficiency, structure, and achieving tangible results. Prefers clear goals and practical solutions.\u000a\u000a## DISC Profile Assessment\u000a\u000a**Primary Style:** D - Dominance  \u000a**Secondary Style:** C - Conscientiousness\u000a\u000a| Style | Estimated % | Evidence |\u000a|-------|-------------|----------|\u000a| D - Dominance | 40% | The leadership role and focus on business development indicate a results-oriented and decisive approach. |\u000a| I - Influence | 20% | Limited evidence of people-oriented behavior, but some engagement in conferences suggests occasional influence traits. |\u000a| S - Steadiness | 10% | Little evidence of patient or consistent behavior given the dynamic nature of the role. |\u000a| C - Conscientiousness | 30% | The detail-oriented nature of SBA lending and structured banking processes suggest a strong focus on accuracy and quality. |\u000a\u000a## Communication Playbook\u000a\u000a### ✅ DO: How to Engage This Person\u000a- Be direct and concise in communication.\u000a- Present clear, logical arguments and data-driven insights.\u000a- Focus on practical outcomes and efficiency.\u000a\u000a### ❌ DON''T: What to Avoid\u000a- Avoid vague or overly abstract ideas without concrete evidence.\u000a- Do not approach with overly emotional appeals or without a clear agenda.\u000a- Steer clear of disorganized or unstructured communication.\u000a\u000a### 🎯 Best Opening Approach\u000aInitiate contact with a clear agenda and specific objectives. Highlight mutual goals or benefits, and be prepared with data or examples to support your points.'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-04T18:55:05.543433','Nur','Nadir',NULL,'http://www.americanbb.bank','Los Angeles','California','Banking',0,'99019736356',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
