INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4756,'Andrew Chambers','achambers@sccountybank.com','+1 818-212-2250','Santa Cruz County Bank',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrew-chambers-b3785840',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-03T17:41:01.924376','2025-12-03T17:41:01.924377',NULL,NULL,NULL,'(707) 293-5167','Vice President at Santa Cruz County Bank','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.924376',0,NULL,unistr('=== PERSON RESEARCH: Andrew Chambers ===\u000a\u000a### 1. Current Role, Responsibilities, and Tenure\u000a\u000a**Current Role:**  \u000aAndrew Chambers is Vice President, SBA Business Development Officer at Santa Cruz County Bank.\u000a\u000a**Responsibilities:**  \u000a- Leads SBA (Small Business Administration) lending initiatives for the bank.\u000a- Focuses on SBA 7(a) and USDA B&I (Business & Industry) loan programs.\u000a- Covers the San Francisco Bay Area and extends north to the Oregon border.\u000a- Works directly with small business owners to structure SBA loans for acquisition, expansion, and modernization.\u000a- Provides guidance on loan packaging, underwriting, and compliance with SBA and USDA requirements.\u000a- Manages client relationships and ensures efficient loan processing from application to funding.\u000a\u000a**Tenure:**  \u000a- Joined Santa Cruz County Bank in 2023 (exact start date not publicly disclosed, but multiple sources confirm his appointment in late 2023).\u000a- Based in Windsor, California, with a regional focus on Northern California.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 2. Complete Career History with Dates and Previous Positions\u000a\u000a- **Santa Cruz County Bank**  \u000a  - **Vice President, SBA Business Development Officer**  \u000a    - 2023–Present  \u000a    - Responsible for SBA and USDA lending in Northern California.\u000a\u000a- **West Coast Community Bank**  \u000a  - **Vice President, SBA, B&I, & FSA Business Development Officer**  \u000a    - 2018–2023  \u000a    - Managed SBA, USDA B&I, and Farm Service Agency (FSA) lending programs.\u000a    - Focused on small business and agricultural lending in California.\u000a\u000a- **Other Banking Roles (Prior to 2018)**  \u000a  - Held various SBA and conventional lending positions at multiple regional and national banks.\u000a  - Progressed from Underwriter to VP Loan Officer, specializing in SBA and USDA financing.\u000a  - Experience spans the Western United States, with a focus on small business lending.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 3. Education, Degrees, and Credentials\u000a\u000a- **Oregon State University**  \u000a  - Bachelor of Science in Business Administration  \u000a  - Minor in History\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 4. Professional Achievements, Awards, and Recognition\u000a\u000a- Consistently recognized as a top producer in SBA 7(a) and USDA B&I lending.\u000a- Instrumental in expanding SBA lending departments at both Santa Cruz County Bank and West Coast Community Bank.\u000a- Noted for expertise in structuring complex SBA loan packages and navigating regulatory requirements.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 5. LinkedIn Activity, Posts, and Thought Leadership\u000a\u000a- No publicly available LinkedIn profile or posts found as of December 2025.\u000a- No evidence of thought leadership articles or posts on professional platforms.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 6. Speaking Engagements, Podcasts, or Media Appearances\u000a\u000a- No record of speaking engagements, podcasts, or media appearances found as of December 2025.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 7. Professional Associations, Board Positions, and Affiliations\u000a\u000a- No publicly disclosed professional associations, board positions, or affiliations as of December 2025.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 8. Management Style and Professional Philosophy\u000a\u000a- Focuses on client-centric service, ensuring small business owners receive tailored SBA and USDA loan solutions.\u000a- Emphasizes efficiency and compliance in loan processing.\u000a- Known for building strong relationships with clients and colleagues.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### 9. Notable Deals, Projects, or Accomplishments\u000a\u000a- Structured and closed significant SBA 7(a) loans for small business acquisitions and expansions in Northern California.\u000a- Played a key role in expanding SBA lending programs at Santa Cruz County Bank, contributing to the bank’s growth in small business lending.\u000a- Successfully managed complex loan packages, including real estate acquisitions and equipment financing, under SBA and USDA programs.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Additional Details\u000a\u000a- **Contact Information:**  \u000a  - Email: achambers@sccountybank.com  \u000a  - Phone: +1 831-457-5003 or +1 707-293-5167\u000a\u000a- **Location:**  \u000a  - Windsor, California (regional coverage: San Francisco Bay Area to Oregon border)\u000a\u000a- **Bank Headquarters:**  \u000a  - Santa Cruz County Bank, 75 River Street, Santa Cruz, CA 95060\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000aThis comprehensive profile is based on publicly available sources, including bank announcements, professional directories, and industry news.\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH: Santa Cruz County Bank ===\u000a\u000aSanta Cruz County Bank is a locally owned and operated community bank founded in 2004 and headquartered in Santa Cruz, California[4][9]. It employs approximately 150 people and operates eight branches across three counties: Santa Cruz, Monterey, and Santa Clara[4]. As of December 31, 2023, the bank held $1.79 billion in assets[4].\u000a\u000a**Business Model and Revenue Generation**  \u000aSanta Cruz County Bank operates as a full-service community bank, focusing on reinvesting deposited funds within the local counties it serves. Its revenue primarily comes from interest on loans, fees for banking services, and Small Business Administration (SBA) lending, where it ranks as one of the region’s top SBA lenders[4]. The bank emphasizes personalized service and customized loans, leveraging its local presence to build strong community ties[4].\u000a\u000a**Products, Services, and Offerings**  \u000aThe bank offers a range of financial products typical of community banks, including:  \u000a- Commercial and residential loans  \u000a- SBA loans  \u000a- Deposit accounts (checking, savings, CDs)  \u000a- Treasury management services  \u000a- Online and mobile banking services  \u000a- Business banking solutions tailored to small and medium enterprises[4][7]\u000a\u000a**Target Markets and Customer Segments**  \u000aSanta Cruz County Bank targets local businesses and residents within Santa Cruz, Monterey, and Santa Clara counties. Its customer base is diversified, including small businesses, agricultural enterprises, and individuals seeking personalized banking services. The bank’s focus on community reinvestment and local economic development differentiates it from larger regional or national banks[4].\u000a\u000a**Competitive Landscape and Differentiation**  \u000aKey competitors include larger regional banks and other community banks in the Bay Area and Central Coast regions. Santa Cruz County Bank differentiates itself through:  \u000a- Strong local community ties and personalized customer service  \u000a- Focus on SBA lending and small business support  \u000a- Reinvestment of deposits within its service area to support local economic growth  \u000a- A diversified customer base that reduces exposure to volatile sectors like high-tech startups, unlike banks such as Silicon Valley Bank[4]\u000a\u000a**Recent News and Strategic Moves**  \u000a- In 2019, Santa Cruz County Bank merged with Lighthouse Bank, another Santa Cruz-based community bank, expanding its branch network and market presence[4].  \u000a- The bank opened a new branch in Salinas, Monterey County, in 2023, expanding its footprint in that region[4].  \u000a- It has grown its loan portfolio in Silicon Valley by nearly $60 million recently, indicating strategic growth in neighboring markets[4].  \u000a- The bank remains headquartered in Santa Cruz and maintains a strong community focus despite expansion[4].  \u000a- West Coast Community Bancorp, the bank’s holding company, held its 2025 annual shareholder meeting in Santa Cruz, indicating ongoing corporate governance and community engagement[5].\u000a\u000a**Company Culture, Values, and Mission**  \u000aSanta Cruz County Bank emphasizes local ownership, community reinvestment, and personalized service. Its mission centers on supporting local businesses and residents by providing customized financial solutions and fostering economic growth within the counties it serves[4]. The bank’s culture values strong community connections and sustainable growth.\u000a\u000a**Leadership Team and Organizational Structure**  \u000aThe bank is led by a management team and board of directors with deep local ties and banking experience. Notable leadership includes individuals with backgrounds in community banking, real estate, and financial services. The organizational structure supports local decision-making and community engagement[5].\u000a\u000a**Technology Stack and Innovations**  \u000aWhile specific technology details are not publicly detailed, Santa Cruz County Bank offers online and mobile banking services, indicating adoption of standard digital banking platforms. Its focus on personalized service suggests a blend of technology and human interaction to meet customer needs[4][7].\u000a\u000a**Market Position, Growth Trajectory, and Industry Standing**  \u000aSanta Cruz County Bank is positioned as a leading community bank in its region, with $1.79 billion in assets and a growing branch network. Its growth trajectory includes geographic expansion into Monterey and Silicon Valley markets and increased loan portfolio diversification. The bank is recognized for stability and community focus, especially in contrast to more volatile regional banks[4]. It ranks among the top SBA lenders in the area, underscoring its importance to local small businesses[4].\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a**Historical Note:**  \u000aThe original County Bank of Santa Cruz was founded in 1870 and operated until 1987, when it merged and eventually became part of Pacific Western Bank and later Comerica Bank. The current Santa Cruz County Bank founded in 2004 is unrelated to that historic institution[1][2][3].\u000a\u000aThis comprehensive profile reflects the current Santa Cruz County Bank as a community-focused, locally owned financial institution with a strong regional presence and commitment to supporting local economies through personalized banking services.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE ===\u000a\u000a### Sales Opportunity Analysis: Andrew Chambers, Vice President, Santa Cruz County Bank\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 1. **Industry Trends Affecting Santa Cruz County Bank’s Sector**\u000a\u000a- **Consolidation and M&A Activity:** The regional banking sector is experiencing increased merger and acquisition activity, driven by the need for scale, efficiency, and digital transformation. Santa Cruz County Bank (now West Coast Community Bank) recently completed a merger with 1st Capital Bancorp, which has significantly expanded its asset base and market reach.\u000a- **Digital Transformation:** Banks are investing heavily in digital banking platforms, mobile apps, and online customer service to meet evolving consumer expectations and compete with fintechs.\u000a- **Rising Interest Rates:** The Federal Reserve has maintained higher interest rates, which has increased net interest margins for banks but also raised funding costs and impacted loan demand.\u000a- **Customer Experience Focus:** There is a growing emphasis on personalized banking experiences, customer retention, and loyalty programs to differentiate from larger national banks.\u000a- **Sustainability and ESG:** Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) initiatives are becoming more important, with banks increasingly expected to demonstrate their commitment to sustainability and community impact.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 2. **Common Pain Points for Vice President at Santa Cruz County Bank Roles**\u000a\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** Balancing the need for cost control with the demand for high-quality customer service.\u000a- **Regulatory Compliance:** Navigating complex and ever-changing regulatory requirements, including those related to anti-money laundering (AML), know your customer (KYC), and data privacy.\u000a- **Technology Integration:** Integrating new technologies and systems while maintaining legacy infrastructure and ensuring data security.\u000a- **Talent Management:** Attracting and retaining skilled professionals in a competitive job market.\u000a- **Customer Retention:** Maintaining strong relationships with existing customers and attracting new ones in a crowded market.\u000a- **Risk Management:** Managing credit risk, operational risk, and reputational risk in a rapidly changing environment.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 3. **Regulatory or Compliance Challenges**\u000a\u000a- **Anti-Money Laundering (AML) and Know Your Customer (KYC):** Ensuring robust AML and KYC processes to prevent financial crimes and comply with federal regulations.\u000a- **Data Privacy and Security:** Adhering to data protection laws such as the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) and the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) for any international operations.\u000a- **Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) Regulations:** Complying with CFPB rules on fair lending, consumer protection, and transparency.\u000a- **Basel III and Capital Requirements:** Meeting capital adequacy and liquidity requirements set by regulatory bodies.\u000a- **Cybersecurity:** Protecting against cyber threats and ensuring the security of customer data and financial transactions.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 4. **Technology Trends Impacting Their Business**\u000a\u000a- **Digital Banking Platforms:** Investment in user-friendly online and mobile banking solutions to enhance customer experience and reduce branch traffic.\u000a- **Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML):** Using AI and ML for fraud detection, risk assessment, and personalized marketing.\u000a- **Blockchain and Distributed Ledger Technology (DLT):** Exploring blockchain for secure and transparent transactions, particularly in areas like supply chain finance and cross-border payments.\u000a- **Cloud Computing:** Migrating to cloud-based solutions for scalability, flexibility, and cost savings.\u000a- **Open Banking:** Embracing open banking APIs to facilitate data sharing and collaboration with fintech partners.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 5. **Economic Factors Affecting Their Decision-Making**\u000a\u000a- **Interest Rate Environment:** Higher interest rates can increase net interest margins but also raise funding costs and impact loan demand.\u000a- **Economic Growth:** Strong economic growth can boost loan demand and deposit growth, while economic downturns can lead to increased credit risk and reduced lending activity.\u000a- **Inflation:** Inflation can erode purchasing power and impact consumer spending, affecting loan and deposit volumes.\u000a- **Housing Market:** The health of the local housing market can influence mortgage lending and real estate-related activities.\u000a- **Unemployment Rates:** High unemployment can lead to increased loan defaults and reduced consumer spending.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 6. **Recent Company Challenges or Opportunities in the News**\u000a\u000a- **Merger with 1st Capital Bancorp:** The recent merger has expanded the bank''s asset base and market reach, but also presents integration challenges and the need for cultural alignment.\u000a- **Record Deposits and Earnings:** The bank reported record deposits of $2.4 billion and strong earnings, indicating robust financial performance and customer confidence.\u000a- **Recognition as a Best Regional Bank:** Santa Cruz County Bank was named one of America''s Best Regional Banks 2025 by Newsweek, highlighting its reputation for customer service and community impact.\u000a- **Strategic Sale of Investments:** The bank has strategically sold investments to optimize its portfolio and improve liquidity.\u000a- **PPP Support During the Pandemic:** The bank provided significant support through the Paycheck Protection Program (PPP), helping to save thousands of jobs and strengthen community ties.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 7. **Typical Buying Triggers for This Persona Type**\u000a\u000a- **Regulatory Changes:** New or updated regulations that require changes to systems, processes, or compliance measures.\u000a- **Technology Upgrades:** The need to modernize legacy systems, improve digital capabilities, or enhance cybersecurity.\u000a- **Mergers and Acquisitions:** Integration of new systems, processes, and cultures following a merger or acquisition.\u000a- **Customer Demand:** Increasing customer expectations for digital services, personalized experiences, and faster transaction times.\u000a- **Operational Efficiency:** The need to reduce costs, streamline processes, and improve operational efficiency.\u000a- **Risk Management:** The need to strengthen risk management and compliance frameworks in response to new threats or regulatory requirements.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 8. **Budget Cycles and Procurement Patterns in This Industry**\u000a\u000a- **Annual Budgeting:** Most banks follow an annual budgeting cycle, with major technology and infrastructure investments typically planned and approved in the first quarter of the year.\u000a- **Procurement Processes:** Procurement is often centralized, with a dedicated procurement team responsible for evaluating and selecting vendors. The process can be lengthy, involving multiple stakeholders and rigorous due diligence.\u000a- **Vendor Relationships:** Banks often prefer to work with established vendors with a proven track record and strong references.\u000a- **Pilot Programs:** Many banks start with pilot programs or proof-of-concept projects before committing to large-scale implementations.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 9. **Key Initiatives Companies Like Santa Cruz County Bank Are Prioritizing**\u000a\u000a- **Digital Transformation:** Investing in digital banking platforms, mobile apps, and online customer service.\u000a- **Customer Experience:** Enhancing customer experience through personalized services, loyalty programs, and omnichannel support.\u000a- **Risk Management:** Strengthening risk management and compliance frameworks to address regulatory and operational risks.\u000a- **Community Engagement:** Building strong relationships with the local community through sponsorships, charitable activities, and community development initiatives.\u000a- **Sustainability and ESG:** Demonstrating a commitment to sustainability and ESG principles through green lending, community impact projects, and transparent reporting.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a#### 10. **Competitive Pressures They''re Facing**\u000a\u000a- **National Banks:** Competition from large national banks with extensive resources and brand recognition.\u000a- **Fintechs:** Competition from fintech companies offering innovative digital banking solutions and lower fees.\u000a- **Regional Banks:** Competition from other regional banks in the same market, particularly those with similar customer bases and service offerings.\u000a- **Customer Expectations:** Increasing customer expectations for digital services, personalized experiences, and fast, secure transactions.\u000a- **Regulatory Environment:** Navigating a complex and evolving regulatory landscape, which can create additional costs and operational challenges.\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a### Actionable Intelligence for Sales Engagement\u000a\u000a- **Highlight Regulatory Compliance Solutions:** Emphasize how your products or services can help Santa Cruz County Bank meet regulatory requirements and reduce compliance risks.\u000a- **Demonstrate Digital Transformation Capabilities:** Showcase your expertise in digital banking platforms, mobile apps, and online customer service to help the bank enhance its digital capabilities.\u000a- **Offer Risk Management and Cybersecurity Solutions:** Highlight your solutions for risk management, fraud detection, and cybersecurity to help the bank protect its assets and customer data.\u000a- **Provide Case Studies and References:** Share case studies and references from other regional banks that have successfully implemented your solutions.\u000a- **Propose Pilot Programs:** Suggest starting with a pilot program or proof-of-concept project to demonstrate the value of your solutions and build trust.\u000a- **Focus on Customer Experience:** Emphasize how your solutions can improve customer experience and help the bank retain and attract customers.\u000a- **Address Operational Efficiency:** Highlight how your solutions can help the bank reduce costs, streamline processes, and improve operational efficiency.\u000a- **Leverage Community Impact:** If applicable, highlight how your solutions can support the bank''s community engagement and sustainability initiatives.\u000a\u000aBy addressing these key areas, you can position your offerings as valuable solutions that align with the strategic priorities and pain points of Andrew Chambers and Santa Cruz County Bank.\u000a\u000a\u000a=== PERSONALITY ANALYSIS ===\u000a\u000a## Myers-Briggs (MBTI) Assessment\u000a\u000a**Inferred Type:** ESTJ  \u000a**Confidence:** Medium\u000a\u000a| Dimension | Preference | Evidence from Research |\u000a|-----------|------------|----------------------|\u000a| Energy | E - Extraversion | Andrew''s role involves extensive interaction with clients and managing relationships, indicating a preference for engaging with others. |\u000a| Information | S - Sensing | His focus on compliance, loan structuring, and regulatory requirements suggests a detail-oriented and practical approach. |\u000a| Decisions | T - Thinking | The emphasis on efficiency, compliance, and structuring complex loan packages points to a logical, objective decision-making style. |\u000a| Structure | J - Judging | Andrew''s role in managing processes from application to funding and ensuring efficiency indicates a preference for structure and organization. |\u000a\u000a**Work Style:** Likely approaches work with a focus on efficiency, structure, and practical solutions. Enjoys managing processes and ensuring compliance with regulations.\u000a\u000a## DISC Profile Assessment\u000a\u000a**Primary Style:** C - Conscientiousness  \u000a**Secondary Style:** D - Dominance\u000a\u000a| Style | Estimated % | Evidence |\u000a|-------|-------------|----------|\u000a| D - Dominance | 25% | Evidence of leading initiatives and expanding lending programs shows a results-oriented approach. |\u000a| I - Influence | 15% | Limited evidence of public speaking or thought leadership, indicating lower emphasis on influence. |\u000a| S - Steadiness | 20% | Building strong client relationships reflects steadiness, but not the primary focus. |\u000a| C - Conscientiousness | 40% | Focus on compliance, structuring complex loans, and managing processes indicates a strong detail-oriented and analytical approach. |\u000a\u000a## Communication Playbook\u000a\u000a### ✅ DO: How to Engage This Person\u000a- Be clear and concise in communication, focusing on facts and data.\u000a- Highlight efficiency and compliance in proposals or discussions.\u000a- Provide structured information and detailed plans.\u000a\u000a### ❌ DON''T: What to Avoid\u000a- Avoid vague or overly abstract ideas without practical application.\u000a- Don''t overlook details or skip steps in processes.\u000a- Avoid overly emotional appeals without logical backing.\u000a\u000a### 🎯 Best Opening Approach\u000aStart with a structured agenda and clear objectives. Emphasize how your proposal aligns with regulatory standards and efficiency improvements.'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-04T18:33:56.690254','Andrew','Chambers',NULL,'','Santa Rosa','','Banking',0,'130938532367',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL);
