INSERT INTO contacts VALUES(4788,'Greg DILL,  - "The SBA Lender','greg@thesbalender.com','949-274-9442','The SBA Lender - Greg Dill',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/gregorysdill',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'completed',NULL,NULL,'2025-12-05T14:24:00.567362','2025-12-03T17:41:01.928652','2025-12-03T17:41:01.928653',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','949-274-9442 | Commercial Real Estate (CRE) | M&A (Biz Acq/Partner Buyout) | 10% Down | SBA 7(a)/7a, 504 & Conventional','hubspot','2025-12-03T17:41:01.928651',0,NULL,unistr('# COMPREHENSIVE SALES INTELLIGENCE PROFILE: GREGORY DILL\u000a\u000a## EXECUTIVE SUMMARY\u000a\u000aGregory Dill operates as a specialized commercial lending professional positioned at the intersection of small business financing, commercial real estate (CRE), and mergers & acquisitions (M&A) transactions. His professional identity centers on serving business owners navigating complex financing scenarios for property acquisitions, business buyouts, and strategic growth initiatives. As a Commercial Mortgage Strategist and SBA Lending Partner, Dill has established himself as a "best in class lending professional" focused on delivering tailored financing solutions to entrepreneurs and business owners seeking capital for transformational transactions.[1][2]\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a## GREGORY DILL - PROFESSIONAL PROFILE\u000a\u000a### Overview\u000a\u000a**Current Role & Organization**\u000a\u000aGregory Dill holds the position of Commercial Mortgage Strategist and Real Estate, Mergers & Acquisitions & SBA Lending Partner at US Bank, one of the nation''s largest financial institutions.[1] His role encompasses multiple specialized lending functions, indicating a senior-level position with significant responsibility across multiple product lines and customer segments. The breadth of his title suggests he operates with considerable autonomy and cross-functional authority within the organization.\u000a\u000a**Key Responsibilities & Areas of Focus**\u000a\u000aDill''s professional mandate centers on four primary areas:\u000a\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate (CRE) Financing:** Structuring and originating commercial mortgage products for business owners seeking to acquire, refinance, or develop real property\u000a- **Business Acquisition & Buyout Financing:** Providing M&A lending solutions for entrepreneurs executing business purchases, leveraged buyouts, and strategic acquisitions\u000a- **SBA Lending Programs:** Leveraging Small Business Administration loan programs (7(a), 504, and other products) to provide accessible financing to small and mid-market businesses\u000a- **Strategic Relationship Management:** Building and maintaining relationships with business owners, commercial brokers, accountants, and other referral sources\u000a\u000aHis positioning as a "Mergers Acquisitions & SBA Lending Partner" indicates he serves as a subject matter expert and go-to resource for complex transaction financing, suggesting he likely mentors junior lenders, develops lending strategies, and handles high-complexity deals.\u000a\u000a**Reporting Structure & Team Dynamics**\u000a\u000aWhile specific reporting structure details are not available in current data, Dill''s role at US Bank places him within the Commercial Banking division. His specialization in multiple lending products suggests he likely operates with a degree of independence while coordinating with underwriting, credit analysis, and loan documentation teams. The "Partner" designation in his title implies he may have some business development responsibilities and potentially manages a portfolio of relationships.\u000a\u000a### Background & Experience\u000a\u000a**Career Trajectory**\u000a\u000aGregory Dill''s professional background reflects deep specialization in commercial lending and real estate finance. His current position at US Bank represents a significant institutional affiliation, as US Bank ranks among the top five commercial lenders in the United States with substantial CRE and SBA lending platforms.[1]\u000a\u000a**Core Competencies & Specializations**\u000a\u000aDill''s expertise encompasses:\u000a\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate Lending:** Demonstrated proficiency in underwriting, structuring, and closing commercial mortgage transactions across multiple property types and borrower profiles\u000a- **SBA Lending Programs:** Deep knowledge of SBA 7(a) loans, 504 loans, and other government-backed lending programs, including program requirements, documentation, and compliance\u000a- **M&A Transaction Finance:** Experience structuring acquisition financing, seller note coordination, earnout provisions, and working capital facilities for business purchases\u000a- **Business Valuation & Analysis:** Capability to evaluate business cash flows, owner compensation, and transaction economics to determine appropriate loan structures and terms\u000a- **Relationship Development:** Proven ability to build and maintain referral networks with commercial real estate brokers, business brokers, accountants, and other professional advisors\u000a\u000a**Professional Positioning**\u000a\u000aDill is positioned as a "best in class lending professional" according to professional directory listings, indicating recognition within the lending community for excellence in client service, deal execution, and market knowledge.[2] This positioning suggests he likely has a track record of successful transactions, strong client satisfaction metrics, and a reputation for solving complex financing challenges.\u000a\u000a### Education & Credentials\u000a\u000a**Formal Education**\u000a\u000aSpecific degree information and educational institutions are not available in current data sources. However, his position at a major financial institution and specialization in complex lending products suggests formal business or finance education, likely including undergraduate degree work in business, finance, economics, or related fields.\u000a\u000a**Professional Certifications & Credentials**\u000a\u000aWhile specific certifications are not documented in available data, lending professionals at Dill''s level typically hold credentials such as:\u000a\u000a- Certified Commercial Investment Member (CCIM) or similar real estate credentials\u000a- SBA lending certifications through the Small Business Administration\u000a- Mortgage Bankers Association (MBA) credentials\u000a- Continuing education in commercial lending and real estate finance\u000a\u000a**Ongoing Professional Development**\u000a\u000aDill''s specialization in multiple lending products and transaction types indicates ongoing engagement with professional development, market education, and industry updates. His role likely requires regular participation in training on regulatory changes, product updates, and market trends affecting commercial lending.\u000a\u000a### Professional Strengths & Working Style\u000a\u000a**Inferred Leadership Approach**\u000a\u000aBased on his positioning as a "Partner" in multiple lending specialties, Dill likely employs a consultative, relationship-driven approach to client engagement. His focus on serving business owners navigating complex transactions suggests he prioritizes understanding client objectives, structuring creative solutions, and providing strategic guidance beyond basic loan origination.\u000a\u000a**Communication Style & Client Engagement**\u000a\u000aDill''s professional profile indicates a client-centric communication approach. His specialization in multiple product areas suggests he can translate complex lending concepts into understandable terms for business owners and their advisors. His role as a "Partner" implies he likely takes an advisory stance, positioning himself as a trusted resource rather than a transactional lender.\u000a\u000a**Decision-Making Patterns**\u000a\u000aHis position at US Bank, combined with his specialization in complex transactions, suggests Dill operates with significant decision-making authority within established lending parameters. His ability to structure deals across multiple product types indicates he likely has authority to approve loan structures, pricing, and terms within defined guidelines, with escalation paths for non-standard requests.\u000a\u000a**Core Values & Professional Motivations**\u000a\u000aDill''s career focus on serving business owners and entrepreneurs suggests alignment with values of business growth, wealth creation, and economic opportunity. His specialization in SBA lending—a program specifically designed to serve small businesses—indicates potential commitment to supporting entrepreneurship and small business development.\u000a\u000a### Social Presence & Professional Engagement\u000a\u000a**Digital Footprint & Online Presence**\u000a\u000aProfessional directory listings indicate Dill maintains a presence on ZoomInfo and Clutch, professional platforms used by business owners and advisors seeking lending professionals.[1][2] These directory listings serve as passive marketing channels, allowing potential clients to discover his services through professional searches.\u000a\u000a**LinkedIn Activity & Thought Leadership**\u000a\u000aWhile specific LinkedIn activity data is not available, lending professionals at Dill''s level typically use LinkedIn for:\u000a\u000a- Relationship maintenance with existing clients and referral sources\u000a- Thought leadership content on commercial lending trends, SBA programs, and transaction structures\u000a- Industry news sharing and market commentary\u000a- Professional networking and business development\u000a\u000a**Speaking Engagements & Industry Participation**\u000a\u000aSpecific speaking engagements or conference appearances are not documented in available data. However, lending professionals with Dill''s specialization often participate in:\u000a\u000a- Small business development seminars and workshops\u000a- Commercial real estate industry events\u000a- Lender panels and financing discussions\u000a- Professional association meetings and networking events\u000a\u000a**Media Presence & Published Content**\u000a\u000aNo specific published articles or media mentions are available in current data. However, professionals in Dill''s position may contribute to:\u000a\u000a- Lending industry publications and blogs\u000a- Commercial real estate market commentary\u000a- SBA program updates and guidance\u000a- Business acquisition and financing guides\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a## THE SBA LENDER - GREG DILL: COMPANY INTELLIGENCE\u000a\u000a### Business Model & Market Position\u000a\u000a**Service Delivery Model**\u000a\u000a"The SBA Lender - Greg Dill" operates as a specialized lending practice focused on providing SBA and commercial lending solutions to business owners. The business model centers on:\u000a\u000a- **Direct Client Service:** Working directly with business owners seeking financing for acquisitions, refinancing, or expansion\u000a- **Referral-Based Development:** Building relationships with commercial brokers, business brokers, accountants, and other professional advisors who refer clients\u000a- **Transaction-Based Revenue:** Generating revenue through loan origination fees, closing costs, and other transaction-related compensation\u000a- **Relationship Banking:** Maintaining long-term client relationships to support multiple transactions and refinancing needs over time\u000a\u000a**Value Proposition**\u000a\u000aThe core value proposition centers on providing "best in class" lending services that help business owners access capital for commercial real estate and business acquisition transactions. This positioning emphasizes:\u000a\u000a- **Expertise & Specialization:** Deep knowledge of SBA programs, commercial lending, and transaction structures\u000a- **Client-Centric Approach:** Focus on understanding client objectives and structuring solutions aligned with business goals\u000a- **Access to Capital:** Leveraging institutional resources and lending programs to provide financing options available to business owners\u000a- **Advisory Partnership:** Positioning as a strategic advisor rather than a transactional lender\u000a\u000a### Products & Services\u000a\u000a**Core Lending Products**\u000a\u000aThe service portfolio encompasses:\u000a\u000a- **SBA 7(a) Loans:** Traditional Small Business Administration loans used for business acquisitions, working capital, equipment, and real estate, with loan amounts up to $5 million\u000a- **SBA 504 Loans:** Specialized real estate and equipment financing through Certified Development Companies, typically used for owner-occupied commercial real estate\u000a- **Commercial Mortgages:** Traditional commercial real estate financing for business owners seeking to acquire or refinance commercial properties\u000a- **Business Acquisition Financing:** Structured lending for business purchases, including acquisition loans, seller note coordination, and working capital facilities\u000a- **Refinancing Solutions:** Refinancing existing commercial mortgages and business loans to improve terms, access equity, or consolidate debt\u000a\u000a**Target Markets & Customer Segments**\u000a\u000aPrimary customer segments include:\u000a\u000a- **Business Owners Seeking Growth Capital:** Entrepreneurs looking to acquire businesses, expand operations, or acquire commercial real estate\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate Investors:** Investors seeking financing for commercial property acquisitions and development\u000a- **Small to Mid-Market Businesses:** Companies with revenues typically ranging from $500,000 to $50 million seeking growth capital\u000a- **Professional Advisors'' Clients:** Business owners referred by accountants, business brokers, and commercial real estate brokers\u000a- **Existing Clients:** Repeat customers seeking refinancing, additional facilities, or financing for new transactions\u000a\u000a**Pricing & Go-to-Market Strategy**\u000a\u000aPricing for SBA and commercial lending products is typically structured as:\u000a\u000a- **Loan Origination Fees:** Typically 1-3% of loan amount, varying by product and borrower profile\u000a- **Interest Rates:** Market-based rates tied to prime rate plus spread, varying by product, term, and borrower creditworthiness\u000a- **Closing Costs:** Third-party costs including appraisals, title insurance, legal fees, and other transaction expenses\u000a- **Ongoing Servicing:** Annual servicing fees on outstanding loan balances\u000a\u000aGo-to-market strategy emphasizes:\u000a\u000a- **Referral Development:** Building relationships with professional advisors (accountants, business brokers, commercial brokers) who refer clients\u000a- **Direct Client Outreach:** Marketing to business owners through digital channels, networking, and industry events\u000a- **Institutional Affiliation:** Leveraging US Bank''s brand, resources, and lending capacity to differentiate from independent lenders\u000a- **Thought Leadership:** Positioning as an expert through educational content, speaking engagements, and industry participation\u000a\u000a### Market Position & Competitive Landscape\u000a\u000a**Industry Category**\u000a\u000aDill operates within the commercial lending and SBA lending sectors, serving the broader small business finance market. This market encompasses:\u000a\u000a- **SBA Lending:** Government-backed lending programs administered through approved lenders\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate Finance:** Institutional and non-institutional lenders providing commercial mortgage products\u000a- **Business Acquisition Finance:** Specialized lenders and banks providing M&A financing for small and mid-market transactions\u000a\u000a**Market Size & Opportunity**\u000a\u000aThe addressable market for SBA and commercial lending is substantial:\u000a\u000a- **SBA Lending Market:** The SBA guarantees approximately $30+ billion in loans annually through its lending programs\u000a- **Commercial Real Estate Finance:** The commercial real estate lending market exceeds $2 trillion in outstanding balances\u000a- **Business Acquisition Market:** Thousands of business acquisitions occur annually in the small to mid-market segment, many requiring acquisition financing\u000a\u000a**Competitive Landscape**\u000a\u000aPrimary competitors include:\u000a\u000a- **Large Commercial Banks:** Major institutions like JPMorgan Chase, Bank of America, and Wells Fargo offering comprehensive commercial lending products\u000a- **Regional Banks:** Mid-sized banks with strong commercial lending platforms and local market presence\u000a- **SBA Lenders:** Specialized lenders focused exclusively on SBA lending programs\u000a- **Non-Bank Lenders:** Alternative lenders, private equity firms, and specialty finance companies offering commercial and acquisition financing\u000a- **Independent Loan Officers:** Individual lenders operating independently or through smaller firms\u000a\u000a**Competitive Advantages & Differentiation**\u000a\u000aDill''s positioning offers several competitive advantages:\u000a\u000a- **Institutional Backing:** Access to US Bank''s capital, underwriting resources, and lending capacity\u000a- **Product Breadth:** Ability to offer multiple lending products (SBA 7(a), 504, commercial mortgages, acquisition financing) from a single source\u000a- **Specialization & Expertise:** Deep knowledge of SBA programs and transaction structures, positioning as a specialist rather than a generalist\u000a- **Relationship-Driven Approach:** Focus on understanding client objectives and structuring customized solutions\u000a- **Established Referral Network:** Relationships with professional advisors, brokers, and other referral sources\u000a- **Market Reputation:** Recognition as a "best in class" lending professional within the commercial lending community\u000a\u000a**Market Share & Growth Trajectory**\u000a\u000aSpecific market share and revenue data are not available in current sources. However, Dill''s position at US Bank, combined with his specialization in multiple lending products, suggests he likely maintains a significant portfolio of active clients and a strong pipeline of transaction opportunities.\u000a\u000a### Leadership & Organizational Structure\u000a\u000a**Leadership Profile**\u000a\u000aGregory Dill serves as the primary client-facing leader for his lending practice. His role encompasses business development, client relationship management, and transaction execution. His position at US Bank places him within a larger organizational structure with support from underwriting, credit analysis, loan documentation, and other operational functions.\u000a\u000a**Organizational Support Structure**\u000a\u000aWhile specific organizational details are not available, Dill''s operations likely benefit from:\u000a\u000a- **Underwriting & Credit Analysis:** Professional underwriters and credit analysts evaluating loan applications and structuring terms\u000a- **Loan Documentation:** Legal and documentation specialists preparing loan documents and closing materials\u000a- **Compliance & Regulatory:** Compliance professionals ensuring adherence to SBA program requirements, banking regulations, and lending standards\u000a- **Operations & Servicing:** Operations teams handling loan servicing, payment processing, and account management\u000a- **Sales & Marketing Support:** Marketing and business development resources supporting client acquisition and referral development\u000a\u000a**Company Culture & Values**\u000a\u000aAs part of US Bank, Dill''s practice operates within a culture emphasizing:\u000a\u000a- **Client Service Excellence:** Focus on delivering superior client experiences and outcomes\u000a- **Risk Management:** Prudent underwriting and credit analysis to manage credit risk\u000a- **Regulatory Compliance:** Strict adherence to banking regulations, SBA program requirements, and lending standards\u000a- **Business Growth:** Emphasis on revenue growth, client acquisition, and market expansion\u000a- **Professional Development:** Investment in employee training, development, and career advancement\u000a\u000a### Recent Activity & Strategic Initiatives\u000a\u000a**Current Market Activity**\u000a\u000aThe commercial lending and SBA lending markets have experienced significant activity in recent years:\u000a\u000a- **Interest Rate Environment:** Rising interest rates have impacted commercial lending volumes and borrower demand for financing\u000a- **Economic Conditions:** Economic uncertainty has affected business acquisition activity and commercial real estate investment\u000a- **SBA Program Updates:** Ongoing updates to SBA lending programs, including changes to loan limits, terms, and program requirements\u000a- **Digital Transformation:** Increasing adoption of digital lending platforms and online application processes\u000a\u000a**Institutional Developments at US Bank**\u000a\u000aAs a major commercial lender, US Bank has likely undertaken strategic initiatives including:\u000a\u000a- **Technology Investment:** Digital lending platforms, automated underwriting, and online account management\u000a- **Product Innovation:** New lending products and program enhancements to serve evolving customer needs\u000a- **Market Expansion:** Geographic expansion and market penetration in key commercial lending segments\u000a- **Organizational Restructuring:** Potential reorganization of lending teams and business units to optimize efficiency and market focus\u000a\u000a**Professional Recognition & Industry Standing**\u000a\u000aDill''s recognition as a "best in class lending professional" indicates:\u000a\u000a- **Client Satisfaction:** Strong track record of successful transactions and satisfied clients\u000a- **Market Reputation:** Recognition within the commercial lending and SBA lending communities\u000a- **Professional Credibility:** Established reputation as a trusted advisor and lending expert\u000a- **Referral Generation:** Strong referral network generating consistent client flow\u000a\u000a---\u000a\u000a## SALES OPPORTUNITY ANALYSIS\u000a\u000a### Trigger Events & Urgency Factors\u000a\u000a**Current Market Conditions Creating Opportunity**\u000a\u000aSeveral factors create immediate opportunity for engagement with Gregory Dill:\u000a\u000a**Interest Rate & Economic Environment**\u000a\u000aThe current economic environment presents several triggers:\u000a\u000a- **Refinancing Opportunities:** Existing clients with commercial mortgages or SBA loans may be seeking refinancing opportunities to improve terms or access equity\u000a- **Acquisition Pipeline:** Business owners evaluating acquisitions may require financing solutions, creating demand for Dill''s services\u000a- **Economic Uncertainty:** Uncertain economic conditions may drive business owners to seek strategic financing advice and capital access strategies\u000a- **Market Volatility:** Commercial real estate market volatility may create opportunities for clients seeking to refinance or restructure existing debt\u000a\u000a**Regulatory & Program Changes**\u000a\u000aSBA and commercial lending programs undergo regular updates:\u000a\u000a- **SBA Program Updates:** Changes to SBA loan limits, terms, or program requirements may create opportunities to discuss new options with existing clients\u000a- **Regulatory Changes:** Banking regulation updates may affect lending products, pricing, or underwriting standards\u000a- **Compliance Requirements:** New compliance requirements may necessitate updates to lending processes or documentation\u000a\u000a**Technology & Process Evolution**\u000a\u000aThe lending industry is undergoing digital transformation:\u000a\u000a- **Digital Lending Platforms:** Adoption of new digital lending platforms may improve efficiency and client experience\u000a- **Automation Opportunities:** Automation of underwriting, documentation, and closing processes may reduce turnaround times\u000a- **Client Portal Development:** Online account management and document portals may enhance client experience\u000a\u000a**Seasonal & Cyclical Factors**\u000a\u000aCommercial lending activity follows seasonal and cyclical patterns:\u000a\u000a- **Year-End Planning:** Business owners often evaluate financing needs and capital strategies in Q4\u000a- **Budget Cycle Timing:** Many businesses plan capital expenditures and financing needs during annual budget cycles\u000a- **Tax Planning:** Year-end tax planning may drive refinancing or capital access decisions\u000a- **Fiscal Year Planning:** Businesses may evaluate growth capital needs as they plan for the upcoming fiscal year\u000a\u000a### Pain Points & Business Challenges\u000a\u000a**Role-Specific Pain Points for Commercial Lenders**\u000a\u000aGregory Dill, as a commercial lending professional, likely faces several operational and business challenges:\u000a\u000a**Client Acquisition & Pipeline Development**\u000a\u000a- **Referral Dependency:** Heavy reliance on referrals from brokers and professional advisors creates vulnerability to referral source changes\u000a- **Lead Generation:** Consistent generation of qualified loan opportunities requires ongoing business development effort\u000a- **Market Competition:** Intense competition from other lenders, banks, and alternative financing sources\u000a- **Client Retention:** Maintaining relationships with existing clients to generate repeat business and referrals\u000a\u000a**Operational Efficiency & Process Challenges**\u000a\u000a- **Loan Processing Speed:** Clients expect fast turnaround on loan decisions and closing; slow processes create competitive disadvantage\u000a- **Documentation Complexity:** Commercial lending involves extensive documentation; manual processes create delays and errors\u000a- **Underwriting Consistency:** Ensuring consistent underwriting standards and decision-making across multiple loan officers and products'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1.0,unistr('=== PERSON RESEARCH ===: Greg DILL,  - "The SBA Lender ===\u000a\u000aGreg Dill currently serves as a **Commercial Mortgage Advisor at The SBA Lender**, a role he has held since 2009, specializing in owner-user commercial real estate financing and SBA lending solutions, including SBA 7(a), 504, and conventional loans with up to 90% loan-to-value ratios[1]. Concurrently, since 2016, he holds the position of **Commercial Mortgage Strategist at Union Bank**, focusing on commercial real estate, mergers and acquisitions (M&A), and SBA lending for business acquisitions and partner buyouts[1][2].\u000a\u000aHis **career history** includes prior roles as a Commercial Mortgage Strategist at Bank of the West and Plaza Bank, where he concentrated on business development for commercial real estate transactions. Earlier in his career, he worked in sales strategy and analysis at Alcatel-Lucent and Lehman Brothers, and in corporate finance at Wachovia Securities[1].\u000a\u000aRegarding **education**, Greg Dill holds a **Bachelor of Science in Economics from Duke University**, a **Master’s degree in International Program - Supply Chain Management (SCM) from MIT**, and earned the **Certified Commercial Investment Member (CCIM) designation in 2015**[1].\u000a\u000aWhile specific **achievements** are not detailed in the search results, his long tenure in specialized SBA lending and commercial real estate financing, along with his CCIM designation, indicate recognized expertise in his field[1][3].\u000a\u000aThere is no publicly available information in the search results about his **LinkedIn activity**, **speaking engagements**, or **board positions**.\u000a\u000aHis **management style** is not explicitly described in the sources found.\u000a\u000aIn summary:\u000a\u000a| Aspect               | Details                                                                                  |\u000a|----------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\u000a| Current Role         | Commercial Mortgage Advisor at The SBA Lender (since 2009); Commercial Mortgage Strategist at Union Bank (since 2016) |\u000a| Career History       | Commercial Mortgage Strategist at Bank of the West, Plaza Bank; Sales strategy/analysis at Alcatel-Lucent, Lehman Brothers; Corporate finance at Wachovia Securities |\u000a| Education            | B.S. Economics (Duke University); Master’s in International Program - SCM (MIT); CCIM designation (2015) |\u000a| Achievements         | Longstanding expertise in SBA lending and CRE financing; CCIM designation                 |\u000a| LinkedIn Activity    | Not available from search results                                                        |\u000a| Speaking Engagements | Not available from search results                                                        |\u000a| Board Positions      | Not available from search results                                                        |\u000a| Management Style     | Not available from search results                                                        |\u000a\u000aThis profile reflects the most current and comprehensive information available from the search results.\u000a\u000a=== COMPANY RESEARCH ===: The SBA Lender - Greg Dill ===\u000a\u000a**The SBA Lender - Greg Dill** is a business entity centered around Greg Dill, who serves as a Commercial Mortgage Advisor specializing in SBA lending and owner-user commercial real estate financing. The business focuses on providing financing solutions for commercial real estate (CRE) and business acquisitions, including mergers and acquisitions (M&A), with loan-to-value ratios up to 90%[1][3].\u000a\u000a- **Overview & Founding:** The SBA Lender is closely associated with Greg Dill, who has been active in this role since at least 2009. There is no explicit founding date or formal company history publicly available, but the business appears to be a personal brand or boutique advisory service led by Dill, leveraging his expertise in SBA loans and commercial real estate financing[1][3].\u000a\u000a- **Headquarters:** The exact headquarters location of The SBA Lender is not explicitly stated in the search results. However, Greg Dill’s professional roles have been linked to institutions such as Union Bank and US Bank, which are based in the United States[1][2].\u000a\u000a- **Employees:** There is no clear data on the number of employees working under The SBA Lender brand. The business seems to be primarily driven by Greg Dill himself, possibly operating as a solo or small team advisory service[4][5].\u000a\u000a- **Revenue:** No specific revenue figures are available for The SBA Lender or Greg Dill’s business activities.\u000a\u000a- **Business Model:** The SBA Lender operates as a commercial mortgage advisory and lending consultancy, specializing in SBA loans and commercial real estate financing. The business model revolves around advising business owners on financing options for CRE purchases and business acquisitions, facilitating loan transactions, and structuring deals to maximize loan-to-value ratios[1][3].\u000a\u000a- **Products/Services:** \u000a  - SBA lending solutions for business acquisitions and buyouts\u000a  - Commercial real estate financing for owner-users\u000a  - Mergers and acquisitions financing advisory\u000a  - Commercial mortgage strategy and deal facilitation[1][2][3]\u000a\u000a- **Target Markets:** The primary target market includes business owners seeking financing for commercial real estate purchases and business acquisitions, particularly those interested in SBA-backed loans and owner-user CRE financing[1][3].\u000a\u000a- **Competitors:** While specific competitors are not listed, typical competitors would include other SBA lenders, commercial mortgage brokers, banks offering SBA loans, and financial advisory firms specializing in CRE and business acquisition financing.\u000a\u000a- **Recent News:** No recent news or updates about The SBA Lender or Greg Dill were found in the search results.\u000a\u000a- **Culture:** There is no direct information about the company culture. Given the nature of the business as a specialized advisory service, it likely emphasizes professionalism, expertise in SBA lending, and client-focused service.\u000a\u000a- **Leadership:** Greg Dill is the principal figure and leader of The SBA Lender. He holds a Bachelor of Science in Economics from Duke University, a Master’s degree in International Program - Supply Chain Management from MIT, and a CCIM designation (Certified Commercial Investment Member) awarded in 2015. He also holds roles at Union Bank and has prior experience at Bank of the West, Plaza Bank, Alcatel-Lucent, Lehman Brothers, and Wachovia Securities[1][2].\u000a\u000aIn summary, The SBA Lender is a specialized commercial mortgage advisory business led by Greg Dill, focusing on SBA lending and commercial real estate financing for business owners, with a strong emphasis on owner-user CRE and business acquisition financing. It operates primarily as a personal brand or boutique advisory service without publicly available detailed corporate structure or financial data.\u000a\u000a=== SALES INTELLIGENCE === ===\u000a\u000aFor Greg Dill, "The SBA Lender," specializing in SBA 7(a), 504, and conventional loans for commercial real estate (CRE) and M&A with 10% down, the **sales opportunity analysis** in 2025 includes the following key insights:\u000a\u000a**Industry Trends:**\u000a- SBA 7(a) lending is experiencing near-record high volumes in 2025, with Q2 FY2025 surpassing $10 billion in approvals, driven largely by small-dollar loans under $150,000, which now constitute over half of all 7(a) loans. This indicates strong demand from small businesses for modest capital to support incremental growth, working capital, and operational needs rather than large-scale projects[1][3].\u000a- CRE financing shows signs of easing interest rate volatility, with commercial loan rates stabilizing or slightly declining in key markets like Texas and the Midwest. Multifamily and stabilized core asset loans range roughly 5.0% to 6.2%, while bridge and construction loans remain higher but competitive[2][5].\u000a- SBA 504 loans remain attractive for CRE buyers due to their fixed long-term rates, low down payments (often 10%), and long repayment terms, providing stability and preserving working capital amid economic uncertainty[4].\u000a\u000a**Pain Points:**\u000a- Borrowers face tight underwriting standards and selective financing despite some rate relief, especially for higher-risk or construction loans[2].\u000a- Small businesses often struggle with working capital constraints, driving demand for smaller SBA loans to bridge cash flow gaps and fund incremental investments[1][3].\u000a- CRE investors confront a high-stakes refinancing cycle in 2025, with $544 billion in loans maturing, creating pressure to secure favorable terms amid a competitive lending environment[5].\u000a\u000a**Regulatory Challenges:**\u000a- SBA lending programs continue to be influenced by federal policy and economic conditions, with pandemic-era relief no longer a factor but ongoing SBA support programs maintaining strong loan volumes[1][3].\u000a- Compliance with SBA loan requirements and documentation remains rigorous, requiring lenders like Greg Dill to maintain expertise in SBA regulations and underwriting standards.\u000a\u000a**Technology Trends:**\u000a- Proprietary loan platforms and digital tools (e.g., Terrydale Live) are increasingly used to streamline loan origination and capital stack management, improving borrower experience and lender efficiency[2].\u000a- Data analytics and reporting tools help lenders track SBA loan activity and market trends, enabling more targeted marketing and risk management[7].\u000a\u000a**Buying Triggers:**\u000a- Small businesses and CRE investors are motivated by opportunities to acquire or refinance properties with low down payments and fixed-rate SBA loans to lock in long-term stability[4].\u000a- M&A activity and partner buyouts drive demand for SBA loans to finance business acquisitions and ownership transitions[1][3].\u000a- Working capital needs and equipment purchases also trigger SBA 7(a) loan applications, especially for smaller loan amounts[6].\u000a\u000a**Budget Cycles:**\u000a- Small businesses often align borrowing with fiscal year planning and growth initiatives, with SBA loan approvals peaking in Q1 to Q3 FY2025, reflecting sustained demand throughout the year[1][3].\u000a- CRE investors may time financing around property acquisition cycles and loan maturity dates, especially given the large volume of loans maturing in 2025[5].\u000a\u000a**Competitive Pressures:**\u000a- Banks are pulling back from some CRE lending segments, increasing competition from private debt funds and alternative lenders offering bridge and mezzanine financing[5].\u000a- SBA lenders compete on expertise, speed, and ability to structure loans with low down payments and fixed rates, differentiating from conventional lenders[4][5].\u000a- The surge in small-dollar SBA loans means lenders must efficiently serve a high volume of smaller transactions while managing risk[1][3].\u000a\u000aThis analysis suggests Greg Dill’s focus on SBA 7(a) and 504 loans for CRE and M&A is well-aligned with current market demand, especially for small to mid-sized loans supporting business growth and property acquisition. Emphasizing fixed-rate SBA 504 loans with low down payments and leveraging technology for efficient loan processing can enhance competitive positioning. Awareness of refinancing pressures and selective underwriting will be critical to advising clients effectively[1][2][4][5].\u000a\u000a\u000a=== PERSONALITY ANALYSIS === ===\u000a\u000a## Myers-Briggs (MBTI) Assessment\u000a**Inferred Type:** ENTJ\u000a**Confidence:** Medium\u000a\u000a### Rationale:\u000aGreg Dill''s career trajectory and roles suggest a personality that aligns with the ENTJ type. ENTJs are known for their leadership qualities, strategic thinking, and ability to manage complex projects, which is evident in Dill''s extensive experience in commercial mortgage advising and strategic roles across various banks. His educational background from prestigious institutions like Duke University and MIT further supports a profile of someone who values knowledge and efficiency, traits common in ENTJs.\u000a\u000a## DISC Profile Assessment\u000a**Primary Style:** D - Dominance\u000a**Secondary Style:** C - Conscientiousness\u000a\u000a### Rationale:\u000aGreg Dill''s role as a Commercial Mortgage Advisor and Strategist indicates a strong focus on results, leadership, and decision-making, aligning with the Dominance (D) style. His meticulous approach to structuring deals and his expertise in SBA lending suggest a secondary Conscientiousness (C) style, emphasizing attention to detail and a methodical approach to problem-solving.\u000a\u000a## Communication Playbook\u000a\u000a### ✅ DO: How to Engage\u000a- **Be Direct and Efficient:** Present information clearly and concisely. Focus on the bottom line and how your proposal aligns with his strategic goals.\u000a- **Highlight Expertise:** Acknowledge his extensive experience and expertise in SBA lending and commercial real estate. Use industry-specific language to demonstrate your understanding of the field.\u000a- **Provide Data and Facts:** Support your points with data and factual information. ENTJs and those with a Dominance style appreciate evidence-based discussions.\u000a\u000a### ❌ DON''T: What to Avoid\u000a- **Avoid Being Overly Emotional:** Keep the conversation professional and avoid overly emotional appeals, as they may not resonate with his logical and strategic mindset.\u000a- **Don''t Be Vague:** Avoid ambiguity or lack of clarity in your communication. Be specific about what you are proposing and the expected outcomes.\u000a\u000a### 🎯 Best Opening Approach\u000a- **Start with a Strategic Insight:** Begin the conversation with a relevant insight or trend in the SBA lending or commercial real estate market. This demonstrates your knowledge and sets the stage for a strategic dialogue.\u000a- **Propose a Collaborative Opportunity:** Frame your proposal as a partnership opportunity that leverages both parties'' strengths to achieve mutual goals. This approach aligns with his leadership and strategic orientation.'),NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,'2025-12-05T14:24:00.567350','Greg','DILL,  - "The SBA Lender',NULL,'http://www.linkedin.com/in/GregorySDill','Orange County','California','Real Estate',0,'150874716595',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,78.79999999999999716,'HIGH',65.0,100.0,64.0,NULL,NULL);
